var tafsirData_23 = {
  "1": "\"Successful indeed are the believers:\" opens the chapter of the believers, and the opening is the chapter's whole promise in three words. The word \"successful\" is 'aflaha,' the word of the farmer whose harvest has come through, and the chapter begins with it, and the placement is deliberate: the success is announced before its conditions are described, so that the reader knows the destination before the road, and the word \"indeed\" gives the announcement the force of certainty, and the verse then names the successful: \"the believers,\" and the name is the chapter's answer to every other claim of success, and the commentators note the structure of the opening: the chapter will now describe the believers in a list of qualities, and the list is the definition of the success, and the reader who wants the success reads the list as the recipe, and the recipe begins in the next verse, and the verses that follow are among the most recited descriptions of the believer in the Quran, and the chapter opens with the prize so that the qualities are read as the price, and the price is paid in the verses that follow. The same success is announced elsewhere: \"O believers! Patiently endure, persevere, stand on guard, and be mindful of Allah, so you may be successful.\"\n\nThe verse is one of the Quran's most famous openings, and its fame is in its economy: three words, and the whole of the believer's life is gathered into them, and the reader is told the verdict first and the evidence second, and the evidence is the list that follows, and the list is the chapter's first section, and the section is the description of the ones who will inherit, and the inheritance is the chapter's last word, and the opening and the closing of the chapter hold the same promise, and the promise is the success of the believers, and the verses between the two are the description of the road.",
  "2": "\"those who humble themselves in prayer\" begins the list of the believers' qualities, and the first quality is the humility in the prayer, and the word \"humble\" is 'khashi'un,' the word of the heart that is lowered and still, and the verse says the believers are humble in their prayer, and the humility is named first because the prayer is the first act of the believer, and the commentators explain the meaning of the humility: it is not the lowering of the voice alone but the stillness of the heart, the presence of the mind, and the awareness of the One being addressed, and the verse says the prayer of the believer is filled with this, and the filling is the first mark of the success that the opening announced. The same quality is described elsewhere: \"And seek help through patience and prayer. Indeed, it is a burden except for the humble\", and the verse of the chapter places the humility at the head of the list, and the placement is the teaching: everything that follows in the list grows out of the prayer, and the prayer that lacks the humility lacks its fruit, and the verse says the successful ones are known first by their prayer, and the prayer is known by its humility.\n\nThe verse is the first stroke of the chapter's portrait, and the portrait is built in short lines, one quality per verse, and the shortness is the chapter's method: the qualities are stated without elaboration, and the reader supplies the elaboration from his own prayer, and the verse asks the reader to measure his prayer against the word \"humble\", and the measuring is the first step of the road the chapter describes. The next verse will add the second quality, the avoidance of idle talk, and the list will continue through the qualities until the portrait is complete, and the completeness will be the chapter's definition of the successful.",
  "3": "\"those who avoid idle talk\" adds the second quality, and the word \"idle talk\" is 'laghw,' the word for the speech that has no good in it, the empty and the false and the distracting, and the verse says the believers turn away from it, and the word \"avoid\" is the word of turning aside, and the commentators explain the meaning: the believer's tongue is guarded, and the speech that neither benefits nor worships is left behind, and the avoidance is the second mark of the successful, and the verse places it second, after the prayer, because the tongue is the second battlefield after the heart, and the prayer that humbles the heart trains the tongue, and the trained tongue avoids the idle, and the verse says the two go together. The same quality appears in the descriptions of the believers elsewhere, and the chapter's list keeps the quality in its place: the prayer is the first mark, and the guarded tongue is the second, and the two are the inward and the outward of the same discipline, and the verse says the successful ones have both.\n\nThe verse is also the chapter's teaching on speech, and the teaching is the measure: the believer's words are measured by their good, and the words without good are idle, and the idle is avoided, and the verse places the measure in the list of the believers so that the reader hears the standard before the examples, and the standard is the avoidance, and the avoidance is the second quality of the successful. The next verse will add the third quality, the paying of the alms, and the list will move from the heart and the tongue to the wealth, and the movement is the chapter's way of covering the whole of the believer's life, the inward and the outward and the possession, and the covering will continue until the portrait is complete.",
  "4": "\"those who pay alms-tax\" adds the third quality, and the word \"alms-tax\" is 'zakah,' the word of purification and growth, and the verse says the believers pay it, and the commentators explain the two meanings folded into the word: the giving purifies the giver's wealth and the giver's heart, and the giving is the growth of both, and the verse places the paying of the alms in the list after the prayer and the guarded tongue, and the placement is the chapter's order of the believer's life: the heart prays, the tongue is guarded, and the wealth is purified, and the three together are the first three marks of the successful, and the verse says the successful ones are known by all three, and the three are the inward, the spoken, and the owned, and the chapter has covered the believer's whole circle in three short verses. The same quality is described elsewhere in the Quran's long list of the believers: \"Surely ˹for˺ Muslim men and women, believing men and women, devout men and women, truthful men and women, patient men and women, humble men and women, charitable men and women,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the quality in its place among the others, and the place is the third, after the prayer and the tongue, and the order is the chapter's teaching.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's word on wealth, and the word is the alms: the believer's wealth is purified by the giving, and the giving is the third quality of the successful, and the verse says the success of the opening verse is built on the giving as much as on the prayer, and the reader who wants the success measures his wealth against the word \"pay\", and the measuring is the third step of the road. The next verse will add the fourth quality, the guarding of the chastity, and the list will turn from the wealth to the desire, and the turn will complete the chapter's circle of the believer's life.",
  "5": "\"those who guard their chastity\" adds the fourth quality, and the word \"guard\" is 'hafizun,' the word of protecting and keeping, and the verse says the believers guard their chastity, and the commentators explain the meaning: the believer keeps his desires within the boundary that God set, and the guarding is the protection of the private self, and the verse places the quality fourth in the list, after the prayer and the tongue and the wealth, and the placement is the chapter's order: the heart, the speech, the possession, and now the desire, and the four together are the believer's whole self, and the verse says the successful ones have guarded all four, and the guarding of the desire is the hardest of the four, and the verse does not elaborate, and the brevity is the chapter's trust: the reader knows the boundary, and the verse asks for the guarding, and the guarding is the fourth mark of the successful. The same guarding is commanded elsewhere: \"˹O Prophet!˺ Tell the believing men to lower their gaze and guard their chastity. That is purer for them.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's word on desire, and the word is the guarding, and the guarding is the fourth quality of the successful, and the verse places it in the list without detail because the next verse will state the boundary that makes the guarding possible, and the boundary is the marriage, and the verse after it will state the limit beyond which the guarding becomes transgression, and the three verses together are the chapter's complete teaching on the matter, and the verse here is the first of the three, and the first is the command: guard, and the guarding is the quality of the successful. The next verse will state the lawful side of the guarding, and the statement will complete the teaching.",
  "6": "\"except with their wives or those ˹bondwomen˺ in their possession, for then they are free from blame\" states the boundary of the guarding, and the verse turns the negative of the previous verse into the positive: the guarding of the chastity has a lawful side, and the lawful side is the marriage, and the verse names it plainly, and the commentators explain the two categories: the wives are the women joined by marriage, and the bondwomen are those joined by the bond of rightful possession as it existed in that time, and the verse says the believer who keeps his desire within these two is free from blame, and the word \"free from blame\" is the word of the one who is not reproached, and the verse places the lawful side and the freedom from blame together, and the placing is the teaching: the desire is not evil, and the guarding is not the killing of the desire, and the verse says the desire has a home, and the home is the marriage, and the one who lives in the home is free, and the one who leaves it is the transgressor of the next verse. The same boundary is stated elsewhere: \"except with their wives or those ˹bondwomen˺ in their possession, for then they are free from blame,\" and the repetition of the verse in the Quran marks it as one of the fixed boundaries of the believer's life.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's completion of the teaching on the desire, and the completion is the balance the Quran always keeps: the guarding is commanded, and the lawful side is named, and the naming prevents the two errors that grow around every command of restraint, the error of the one who lets the desire run and the error of the one who imagines the desire itself is the sin, and the verse says the desire is a guest with a home, and the home is the marriage, and the verse after it will name the one who leaves the home, and the naming will complete the chapter's teaching on the matter, and the completion is the fifth quality of the successful.",
  "7": "\"but whoever seeks beyond that are the transgressors\" states the limit of the previous verse, and the verse is the second half of the boundary: the lawful side was named, and now the other side is named, and the word \"beyond\" draws the line, and the word \"transgressors\" is 'adun,' the word of those who cross, and the verse says the one who seeks satisfaction outside the two lawful categories has crossed the boundary, and the crossing is the transgression, and the commentators explain the meaning: the verse condemns every seeking beyond the marriage and the rightful bond, and the condemnation is the chapter's completion of the guarding, and the verse says the successful ones are those who guard, and the guarded are those who stay within, and the ones who cross are the transgressors, and the three verses together are the chapter's complete teaching on the desire: guard it, satisfy it within the lawful, and know that the beyond is the transgression. The same boundary is stated elsewhere: \"Also ˹forbidden are˺ married women—except ˹female˺ captives in your possession. This is Allah’s commandment to you.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's word on the limit, and the word is the transgression, and the verse places the limit after the lawful side so that the reader sees both sides of the line, and the seeing is the protection, and the protection is the fifth quality of the successful, and the verse says the quality is the guarding, and the guarding is the staying within, and the staying within is the freedom from blame that the previous verse named, and the crossing is the transgression that this verse names, and the two names are the two sides of the believer's life, and the chapter has drawn them both, and the next verse will turn to the sixth quality, the keeping of the trusts, and the list will continue from the private self to the public dealings, and the continuation will complete the chapter's circle of the believer.",
  "8": "\"˹the believers are also˺ those who are true to their trusts and covenants\" adds the sixth quality, and the verse turns the list from the private self to the public life, and the two words are the two sides of the believer's reliability: the trusts and the covenants. The word \"trusts\" is 'amanat,' the things placed in a person's keeping, the property and the secrets and the duties, and the word \"covenants\" is 'ahd,' the promises and the agreements, and the verse says the believers are true to both, and the word \"true\" is 'ra'un,' the word of guarding and keeping, and the commentators explain the meaning: the believer returns what was entrusted and keeps what was promised, and the two are the foundation of every society, and the verse places them in the list of the successful because the success of the Hereafter is built on the honesty of this world, and the chapter says the two belong together, the trust that is kept and the promise that is honoured, and the believer is known by both, and the verse is the chapter's word on the public life of the successful, and the word is the keeping, and the keeping is the sixth quality. The same quality is stated in the Quran's descriptions of the believers elsewhere, and the verse of the chapter keeps it in its place in the list, and the place is after the guarding of the chastity, and the order is the chapter's teaching: the private self is guarded, and the public dealings are kept, and the two together are the believer's wholeness.\n\nThe verse is also the chapter's teaching on the foundations of trust, and the teaching is the two words: the trust and the covenant, and the verse says the believer's word is a bond, and the bond is kept, and the keeping is the sixth mark of the successful, and the reader who wants the success measures his word against the verse, and the measuring is the sixth step of the road. The next verse will add the seventh quality, the observance of the prayers, and the list will return to the prayer with which it began, and the return will close the circle of the qualities, and the closing will show that the believer's whole life is framed by the prayer, at the beginning and at the end.",
  "9": "\"and those who are ˹properly˺ observant of their prayers\" adds the seventh and closing quality of the list, and the verse returns to the prayer, and the return is the chapter's design: the list began with the humility in prayer and ends with the observance of the prayers, and the two ends frame the whole list, and the framing is the teaching: the prayer is the beginning and the end of the believer's qualities, and everything between the two ends hangs on the prayer, and the verse says the successful ones observe their prayers, and the word \"observant\" is 'hafizun,' the word of guarding and keeping, and the commentators explain the meaning: the believer keeps the prayers at their times and in their completeness, and the keeping is the seventh quality, and the word \"properly\" is the verse's demand for the full observance, not the hurried performance, and the verse places the observance at the end of the list so that the reader ends where he began, in the prayer, and the ending is the chapter's summary: the believer prays with humility and keeps the prayers with care, and between the two he guards his tongue and pays his alms and protects his chastity and keeps his trusts, and the whole life is the prayer's orbit. The same observance is stated elsewhere in the Quran's lists of the believers, and the verse of the chapter closes its list with the observance, and the closing is the seventh stroke of the portrait.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's completion of the portrait of the successful, and the completion is the list itself: seven qualities in seven short verses, and the seven are the believer's whole life, and the chapter has drawn the life in its shortest lines, and the next verse will announce the reward of the seven, and the announcement will be the chapter's first promise, and the promise will be the inheritance that the chapter's later verses will describe, and the reader who has followed the list has been shown the road, and the next verse will show the destination.",
  "10": "\"These are the ones who will be awarded\" announces the reward of the seven qualities, and the verse is the hinge of the chapter's opening: the list is finished, and the reward is stated, and the word \"these\" points back to the seven verses, and the word \"awarded\" is the word of inheritance, and the verse says the ones of the seven qualities will inherit, and the inheritance is the chapter's first word on the reward, and the commentators note the word's root: the inheritor is the one who receives what is rightfully his, and the verse says the reward is an inheritance, not a favour, and the placing of the word here prepares the chapter's final verse, where the inheritance is named again, and the two verses hold the chapter together, the award at the beginning and the inheritance at the end, and the reader is told at the start what the end will confirm. The verse is one line, and the line is the bridge between the list and the destination, and the bridge is the word \"awarded\", and the next verse will name the inheritance, and the naming will complete the chapter's opening promise, and the promise is the success of the first verse, and the success is the Paradise of the next.\n\nThe verse also teaches the reader the Quran's way with the lists of qualities: the list is never left hanging, and the reward is never delayed past the list, and the verse says \"these\" as soon as the list closes, and the immediacy is the chapter's mercy, and the reader who has measured himself against the seven qualities is given the reward in the same breath, and the reward is the award, and the award is the inheritance, and the next verse will describe the inheritance in its two words, the Paradise and the forever.",
  "11": "\"Paradise as their own. They will be there forever\" names the inheritance, and the verse is the destination of the chapter's opening. The word \"Paradise\" is 'al-Firdaws,' and the commentators explain that it is the highest of the Gardens, and the word comes into Arabic from the ancient tongues, and the verse says the successful ones will inherit it, and the word \"as their own\" completes the award of the previous verse: the Paradise is not a visit but a possession, and the possessors are the seven qualities' people, and the verse then adds the second word: \"They will be there forever\", and the word \"forever\" is 'khalidun,' the word of the endless stay, and the verse says the inheritance has no term, and the two words together are the chapter's whole promise: the Paradise, and the forever, and the promise is the reward of the list that the chapter opened with, and the reader who has followed the seven qualities has been led to the two words, and the two words are the success of the first verse made visible. The same inheritance is described elsewhere: \"Indeed, those who believe and do good will have the Gardens of Paradise as an accommodation,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the inheritance in its place at the head of the chapter, and the place is the prize, and the prize is named before the road continues.\n\nThe verse is the closing of the chapter's first section, and the closing is the pair of words, the Paradise and the forever, and the pair is the Quran's constant description of the reward, and the chapter has placed it at the head so that the reader carries it through the rest of the chapter, and the rest of the chapter will describe the creation of the human being and the stories of the prophets and the scenes of the Day, and the reader who carries the pair through all of it reads everything as the road to the pair, and the next verse will begin the road with the creation of the human being, and the beginning will show the reader where he came from, and the showing is the chapter's second teaching.",
  "12": "\"And indeed, We created humankind from an extract of clay,\" opens the chapter's second section, and the section turns from the qualities of the successful to the creation of the human being, and the turn is the chapter's argument: the one who knows his beginning will know his end, and the verse states the beginning in its first stage, and the word \"extract\" is 'sulalah,' the word of the pure drawn-out part, and the verse says the human being was made from an extract of clay, and the commentators explain the meaning: the beginning of the human being is the earth, and the extract is the essence drawn from it, and the verse places the earth at the head of the human story, and the placement is the teaching: the human being is not a stranger to the ground, and the ground is his origin, and the origin is the chapter's first word on the human condition, and the next verses will follow the origin through its stages, and the stages will be the chapter's proof of the power that made them, and the proof will be the answer to the deniers of the resurrection, and the answer will be the chapter's second argument, and the argument will run through the verses that follow, and the reader will be led from the clay to the grave and beyond.\n\nThe verse is the first stage of the chapter's description of the human creation, and the description is one of the Quran's most detailed, and the detail is the argument: the stages are named one by one, and the naming is the proof, and the verse says the first stage is the clay, and the next verse will name the second, and the naming will continue until the reader has walked the whole road from the earth to the birth, and the walking is the chapter's way of showing the reader the hand that carried him along the road, and the next verse will state the second stage, and the second stage will be the drop, and the drop will be placed in its secure place, and the placement will be the chapter's next stroke.",
  "13": "\"then placed each ˹human˺ as a sperm-drop in a secure place,\" states the second stage of the creation, and the verse moves from the clay to the drop, and the word \"sperm-drop\" is 'nutfah,' the word of the small drop, and the verse says the human being was placed as a drop, and the word \"placed\" is 'ja'alna,' the word of appointing, and the verse says the placement was an appointment, not an accident, and the destination of the drop is named: \"in a secure place,\" and the commentators explain that the secure place is the womb, and the word \"secure\" is 'makin,' the word of the firm and the settled, and the verse says the drop was settled in the womb the way a guest is settled in a protected home, and the settling is the chapter's picture of the care that surrounds the human being at his smallest, and the picture is the argument: the drop that was settled and guarded and developed was never abandoned, and the one who guarded the drop will raise the dead, and the verse says it in the language of the journey, and the journey has just begun, and the next verse will follow the drop through its changes, and the changes will be the chapter's proof of the power that shaped them, and the proof will run to the end of the section, and the section will close with the death and the raising, and the closing will be the chapter's answer to the deniers. The verse is also a word against pride. The human being who struts over the earth began as a drop in a guarded place, and the verse says the beginning was an appointment, and the remembering of the beginning is the cure of the strutting, and the cure is the chapter's gift to every reader who has forgotten what he was.",
  "14": "\"then We developed the drop into a clinging clot ˹of blood˺, then developed the clot into a lump ˹of flesh˺, then developed the lump into bones, then clothed the bones with flesh, then We brought it into being as a new creation. So Blessed is Allah, the Best of Creators\" continues the stages of the creation, and the verse is the longest of the section, and its length is the argument: the stages are named in their order, and the order is the proof, and the verse walks the reader through the whole development, and the commentators explain each stage: the clinging clot is the stage of attachment, and the lump of flesh is the stage of the formless mass, and the bones are the stage of the frame, and the clothing of the bones with flesh is the stage of the body's completion, and the verse then names the final stage: \"then We brought it into being as a new creation\", and the word \"new\" is the verse's key, and the commentators explain its meaning: the completed human being is a new creation, different from every stage before it, and the verse says the new creation was brought into being, and the bringing is the power the whole section is displaying, and the verse then closes with the praise: \"So Blessed is Allah, the Best of Creators\", and the word \"Blessed\" is 'tabaraka,' the word of the abounding good, and the verse says God is blessed and the best of creators, and the praise is the section's answer to every doubt: the stages are the work of the Best of Creators, and the work is the proof of the power, and the proof is the foundation of the resurrection that the chapter will announce. The same stages are described elsewhere: \"then We developed the drop into a clinging clot ˹of blood˺, then developed the clot into a lump ˹of flesh˺, then developed the lump into bones, then clothed the bones with flesh, then We brought it into being as a new creation.\"\n\nThe verse is the heart of the chapter's argument from creation, and the argument is the Quran's method at its fullest: the reader is not asked to believe the resurrection as a bare claim; he is asked to remember his own stages, the drop and the clot and the lump and the bones, and the memory is the proof, and the verse says the proof ends in the praise, and the praise is the reader's response, and the next verse will state the death that follows the life, and the statement will complete the argument, and the completion will be the chapter's transition from the creation to the resurrection, and the transition is the section's destination.",
  "15": "\"After that you will surely die,\" states the next stage of the human journey, and the verse is the shortest of the section, and its shortness is its weight: after the stages of the creation, the death, and the verse states it as the certain end, and the word \"surely\" gives the statement the force of the fixed, and the commentators explain the placement: the creation was described stage by stage, and the death is stated in one line, because the death needs no description, and every reader knows it, and the verse uses the knowledge as the bridge to the next announcement: the one who knows he will die should ask what comes after, and the next verses will answer the question, and the answer is the resurrection, and the verse has prepared the reader for it by walking him from the clay to the drop to the stages to the death, and the walk is the chapter's argument, and the argument is that the One who carried the human being through the first journey will carry him through the second, and the verse says the first journey ends in death, and the chapter will say the death is not the end, and the next verse will state the raising, and the raising will be the chapter's answer to the death, and the answer is the section's completion, and the completion is the Quran's teaching on the human story: the creation, the death, and the raising, and the three are one journey, and the journey's end is the return. The verse is the chapter's calmest statement of the certain thing, and the calm is the teaching: the death that terrifies is a stage among stages, and the verse states it in the same voice that stated the clay and the drop, because the One who spoke the beginning speaks the end, and the believer who reads the verse hears the end as the middle of the road, not its close.",
  "16": "\"then on the Day of Judgment you will be resurrected\" completes the human journey that the previous verses traced, and the verse is the destination of the whole description. The stages were named, the clay and the drop and the clot and the bones, and the death was stated, and now the verse states the final stage: the resurrection, and the verse fixes its time, \"on the Day of Judgment\", and the word \"resurrected\" is the word of being raised and sent forth, and the verse says the raising is certain, and the certainty is the argument of the whole section: the One who carried the human being from the clay to the birth will carry him from the grave to the standing, and the two journeys are one power, and the verse says the second journey is as certain as the first, and the reader who has followed the stages has been led, step by step, to this sentence, and the sentence is the section's completion. The same argument is stated elsewhere: \"And Allah brought you out of the wombs of your mothers while you knew nothing, and gave you hearing, sight, and intellect\", and the verse of the chapter closes the argument with the resurrection, and the closing is the chapter's answer to the deniers who will be quoted in the verses ahead, and the answer is the power that the stages displayed.\n\nThe verse is also the chapter's bridge from the creation to the signs, and the bridge is the resurrection, and the reader who has accepted the journey of the stages is now asked to look at the sky and the rain and the cattle, and the next verses will display the signs, and the display is the chapter's second proof, and the proof is the same as the first: the power that runs the world is the power that raises the dead, and the verse has stated the raising, and the next verses will show the running, and the two together are the chapter's complete argument, and the argument will run through the verses that follow, and the reader will be led from the resurrection to the signs and from the signs to the stories of the prophets, and the leading is the chapter's way.",
  "17": "\"And indeed, We created above you seven levels ˹of heaven˺. We are never unmindful of ˹Our˺ creation\" turns the gaze upward, and the verse states the creation of the heavens in two parts. The first part is the creation: \"seven levels ˹of heaven˺\", and the word \"levels\" is 'tara'iq,' the word of the stacked paths, and the verse says God created seven heavens above the reader, and the commentators explain the number as the Quran's description of the heavens in their multiplicity, and the verse places the seven heavens above the human being as the roof of the world, and the roof is the first of the signs. The second part is the verse's key: \"We are never unmindful of ˹Our˺ creation\", and the word \"unmindful\" is 'ghafilin,' the word of the heedless, and the verse says God is never heedless of what He created, and the statement is the answer to the fear that the creation might be abandoned, and the commentators explain the meaning: the One who created the heavens and the earth did not create them and forget them, and the care that runs the seven heavens is the same care that will run the resurrection, and the verse says the care is constant, and the constancy is the believer's ground, and the verse places the creation and the care together, and the two together are the sign. The same creation is described elsewhere: \"He is the One Who created the heavens and the earth in truth\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the statement in its place in the sequence of the signs, and the place is the first of the signs, the sky above, and the next sign will be the rain below, and the sequence will descend from the heavens to the earth, and the descent is the chapter's order.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's first sign, and the sign is the sky, and the lesson of the sign is the care, and the care is the verse's last word, and the reader who looks up is asked to see not only the roof but the Roofer, and the Roofer is not heedless, and the next verses will show the care in the rain and the plants and the cattle, and the showing will complete the chapter's display of the signs, and the display will prepare the stories of the prophets that follow.",
  "18": "\"We send down rain from the sky in perfect measure, causing it to soak into the earth. And We are surely able to take it away\" states the second sign, and the verse describes the rain with the word that is the verse's whole teaching: \"in perfect measure\". The word \"measure\" is 'qadar,' the word of the fixed amount, and the verse says the rain is sent down in measure, and the commentators explain the meaning: the rain is not sent at random, and the amount is weighed, and the weighing is the sign, because the water that drowns and the water that nourishes are the same water, and the measure is what makes the difference, and the verse says the measure is God's, and the reader is asked to see the measured rain as the signature of the measurer. The verse then adds the second fact: \"causing it to soak into the earth\", and the word \"soak\" is the word of the settling in, and the verse says the rain is settled into the ground, and the settling is the preparation for the plants of the next verse, and the verse then closes with the warning that balances the gift: \"And We are surely able to take it away\", and the word \"surely\" gives the warning its force, and the verse says the One who sent the rain can withdraw it, and the withdrawal is the reminder, and the reminder is the verse's second teaching: the gift is a loan, and the loan can be called in, and the reader who drinks the rain should remember the hand that sent it and the hand that can take it. The same rain is described elsewhere: \"He is the One Who sends down rain from the sky, from which you drink and by which plants grow for your cattle to graze\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the measure at the center, and the center is the chapter's word on the rain, and the word is the measure.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's second sign, and the sign is the rain, and the lesson of the sign is the measure and the power, and the two together are the verse's teaching: the rain is measured, and the rain is held, and the one who reads the two facts has read the sign, and the next verse will show what the measured rain produces, the gardens and the fruits, and the production will be the chapter's third sign, and the signs will continue to the cattle and the ships, and the continuation will complete the chapter's display of the provision, and the display will be the foundation of the stories that follow.",
  "19": "\"With it We produce for you gardens of palm trees and grapevines, in which there are abundant fruits, and from which you may eat\" states the third sign, and the verse turns from the rain to its fruit, and the word \"with it\" ties the gardens to the rain of the previous verse, and the chain is the verse's argument: the rain came down in measure, and the gardens came up from it, and the chain has no break, and the reader is asked to see the chain as the sign. The gardens are named: \"palm trees and grapevines,\" and the two are the trees the first listeners knew best, the date palm and the vine, and the verse names them because they were the wealth of Arabia, and the naming is the verse's way of bringing the sign home, and the verse then adds the abundance: \"in which there are abundant fruits, and from which you may eat,\" and the word \"abundant\" is the word of the many, and the verse says the fruits are many and the eating is permitted, and the permission is the verse's answer to the pagans who had forbidden the fruits of their own invention, and the verse says the fruits are God's gift and the eating is God's permission, and the two together are the sign's completion. The same provision is described elsewhere: \"And We send down blessed rain from the sky, bringing forth gardens and grains for harvest, and towering palm trees ˹loaded˺ with clustered fruit,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the gardens in their place in the sequence, and the place is the third sign, the fruit of the rain, and the next verse will name the fourth tree, the olive of Mount Sinai, and the naming will complete the chapter's list of the provision.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's teaching on the provision, and the teaching is the chain: the rain, the gardens, and the eating, and the verse says the chain is God's, and the reader who eats the fruit is at the end of the chain, and the end is the place of gratitude, and the verse places the eating after the producing so that the eater remembers the producer, and the next verse will add the olive, and the addition will show the variety of the provision, and the variety is the sign, and the signs will continue through the cattle and the ships, and the continuation is the chapter's display of the care that the earlier verse announced.",
  "20": "\"as well as ˹olive˺ trees which grow at Mount Sinai, providing oil and a condiment to eat\" adds the fourth tree to the list of the provision, and the verse names the olive and its mountain, and the naming is the verse's precision. The olive is described as growing \"at Mount Sinai,\" and the commentators explain the meaning: the olive trees of the Sinai region were famous for their oil, and the verse points to the mountain as the place of the blessing, and the pointing is the verse's way of tying the provision to the land, and the land is the reader's own, and the tie is the sign. The verse then names the two gifts of the olive: \"providing oil and a condiment to eat\", and the oil is the gift of the light and the anointing, and the condiment is the gift of the taste, and the verse says the one tree provides both, and the two gifts are the verse's word on the provision's fullness, and the reader is asked to see the olive as the example of every tree, and the example is the sign. The same provision is described elsewhere: \"He is the One Who produces gardens—both cultivated and wild—and palm trees, crops of different flavours, olives, and pomegranates—similar ˹in shape˺, but dissimilar ˹in taste˺,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the olive in its place in the list, and the place is the fourth tree, and the list is the chapter's display of the provision's variety, and the variety is the argument, and the argument is that the One who grew the variety is the One who deserves the worship, and the verse says it without saying it, and the reader supplies the conclusion.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's fourth sign, and the sign is the olive, and the lesson of the sign is the variety, and the variety is the chapter's way of showing the care that runs through the provision, and the next verse will turn from the trees to the cattle, and the turn is the chapter's movement from the plant to the animal, and the movement will continue to the ships, and the continuation will complete the display of the signs, and the display will be the foundation of the stories of Noah and the generations that follow, and the foundation is the provision, and the provision is the chapter's answer to the ingratitude it will describe.",
  "21": "\"And there is certainly a lesson for you in cattle, from whose bellies We give you ˹milk˺ to drink, and in them are many other benefits for you, and from them you may eat\" turns the signs from the plants to the animals, and the verse opens with the word that runs through the chapter: \"a lesson.\" The word \"lesson\" is 'ibrah,' the word of the crossing over, and the verse says the cattle are a lesson, and the commentators explain the word's root: the lesson is the crossing of the mind from the thing seen to the truth behind it, and the verse asks the reader to cross from the cattle to the One who made them, and the crossing is the sign. The first gift is named: \"from whose bellies We give you ˹milk˺ to drink,\" and the verse points to the milk and its place, and the commentators note the wonder folded into the verse: the milk comes from between the waste and the blood, as the Quran says elsewhere, and the verse says the pure drink is drawn from the impure surroundings, and the drawing is the sign, and the sign is the care that separates the pure from the impure for the drinker. The verse then adds the rest of the gifts: \"and in them are many other benefits for you, and from them you may eat\", and the word \"many\" gathers the wool and the hides and the strength and the riding, and the verse says the benefits are many, and the eating is permitted, and the verse places the milk and the benefits and the meat together, and the three are the cattle's gifts, and the gifts are the lesson. The same lesson is stated elsewhere: \"And there is certainly a lesson for you in cattle: We give you to drink of what is in their bellies, from between digested food and blood: pure milk, pleasant to drink,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the lesson in its place in the sequence of the signs, and the place is the fifth sign, the cattle, and the next verse will add the riding, and the addition will complete the chapter's word on the cattle.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's teaching on the animal as a sign, and the teaching is the crossing, and the crossing is the lesson, and the reader who drinks the milk is asked to cross from the drink to the giver, and the crossing is the chapter's method with every sign, and the next verse will add the carrying, and the carrying will show the cattle as the traveler's companion, and the companion is the sign, and the signs will continue to the ships, and the continuation will complete the chapter's display, and the display is the foundation of the stories that follow.",
  "22": "\"And you are carried upon ˹some of˺ them and upon ships\" completes the cattle's gifts with the carrying, and the verse joins the animal and the ship in one sentence, and the joining is the verse's teaching: the cattle carry on the land, and the ships carry on the sea, and the two are one gift, and the gift is the carrying, and the verse says the reader is carried, and the word \"carried\" is the word of the lifting and the bearing, and the verse points to the everyday fact that the reader takes for granted, and the pointing is the sign: the human being crosses the land and the sea on backs that were subjected to him, and the subjection is the gift, and the verse says the gift is God's, and the reader is asked to see the carrying as the lesson that the previous verse announced. The same carrying is described elsewhere: \"It is Allah Who created the heavens and the earth and sends down rain from the sky, causing fruits to grow as a provision for you. He has subjected the ships for your service, sailing through the sea by His command,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the carrying in its place at the end of the signs, and the place is the closing of the display, and the closing is the chapter's summary: the sky, the rain, the gardens, the olive, the cattle, and the ships, and the six are the provision, and the provision is the sign, and the sign is the care that the earlier verse announced, and the care is the chapter's answer to the ingratitude that the next section will describe.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's completion of the signs, and the completion is the carrying, and the carrying is the most ordinary of the signs, and the ordinariness is the verse's point: the signs are not only in the wonders but in the daily ride, and the reader who has been carried on the cattle and the ships has been carried by the gift, and the gift is the sign, and the next verse will turn to the story of Noah, and the turn will move the chapter from the signs of the provision to the stories of the messengers, and the movement is the chapter's design: the provision was shown, and now the response to the provision will be shown, and the response is the denial, and the denial is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "23": "“Indeed, We sent Noah to his people. He declared, “O my people! Worship Allah ˹alone˺. You have no god other than Him. Will you not then fear ˹Him˺?”” records the mission, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the mission: “Indeed, We sent Noah to his people. He declared, ‘O my people! Worship Allah alone. You have no god other than Him. Will you not then fear Him?’” or “wa laqad arsalna Noohan ila qawmihi fa-qala ya qawmi i‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruhu afala tattaqoon,” and the commentators note the mission, for Noah called the worship, and the verse is the record of the mission, the call that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the oneness, and the fear is the appeal, and the verse is the record of the mission, the call of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mission is the call, and the oneness is the message, and the verse is the record of the mission, the call that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worship. The believer is taught that the call of the oneness is the message, and the one who worships the One is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who fears the Lord is the believer whose fear the Lord accepts, and the call of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the oneness is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the call of the oneness: Noah called and was denied, and the believer who worships the One is the believer whose worship the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the oneness is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the mission, and the teaching of the worship that the oneness requires.",
  "24": "“But the disbelieving chiefs of his people said ˹to the masses˺, “This is only a human like you, who wants to be superior to you. Had Allah willed, He could have easily sent down angels ˹instead˺. We have never heard of this in ˹the history of˺ our forefathers.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “But the disbelieving chiefs of his people said to the masses, ‘This is only a human like you, who wants to be superior to you. Had Allah willed, He could have easily sent down angels instead. We have never heard of this in the history of our forefathers,’” or “fa-qala al-mala’u allatheena kafaroo min qawmihi ma hatha illa basharun mithlukum yureedu an yatafaddala ‘alaykum wa law sha’a Allahu la-anzala mala’ikatan ma sami‘na bihatha fee aba’ina al-awwaleen,” and the commentators note the reply, for the chiefs accused Noah, and the reply is the rejection, the three objections, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the three objections are the humanity and the ambition and the novelty, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the rejection, and the three objections are the charges, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the rejection of the truth is the pattern, and the one who is rejected is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the accusation is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the reply of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the rejection is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the pattern of the rejection: Noah was accused and denied, and the believer who bears the accusation is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the rejection is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the patience that the rejection requires.",
  "25": "“He is simply insane, so bear with him for a while.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the reply: “He is simply insane, so bear with him for a while,” or “in huwa illa rajulun bihi jinnatun fa-tarabbasoo bihi hatta heen,” and the commentators note the completion, for the chiefs declared Noah insane, and the completion is the accusation, the label of the madness, and the verse is the record of the completion, the accusation that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the label of the madness is the oldest of the charges, and the verse is the record of the completion, the accusation of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the label, and the patience is the counsel, and the verse is the record of the completion, the accusation that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the label, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the label of the madness is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is labelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the completion of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the label: Noah was labelled and denied, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the patience that the label requires.",
  "26": "“Noah prayed, “My Lord! Help me, because they have denied ˹me˺.”” records the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the prayer. The verse records the prayer: “My Lord! Help me, because they have denied me,” or “qala rabbi ansurnee bima kaththaboon,” and the commentators note the prayer, for Noah sought the help, and the prayer is the recourse, the turning of the prophet to the Lord, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the recourse that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the recourse of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prayer is the recourse, and the help is the request, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the recourse that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the prayer of the denied is the recourse, and the one who seeks the help is the one whose help the Lord grants, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the prayer of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the prayer is the believer whose reliance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the recourse of the denied: Noah prayed and was helped, and the believer who seeks the help is the believer whose help the Lord grants, and the prayer is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the reliance that the denial requires.",
  "27": "“So We inspired him: “Build the Ark under Our ˹watchful˺ Eyes and directions. Then when Our command comes and the oven bursts ˹with water˺, take on board a pair from every species along with your family —except those against whom the decree ˹to drown˺ has already been passed. And do not plead with Me for those who have done wrong, for they will surely be drowned.”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: “So We inspired him: ‘Build the Ark under Our watchful Eyes and directions… for they will surely be drowned,’” or “fa-awhayna ilayhi ani isna‘ al-fulka bi-a‘yunina wa wahyina fa-itha ja’a amruna wa fara at-tannooru fasluk feeha min kullin zawjayni ithnayni wa ahlaka illa man sabaqa ‘alayhi al-qawlu minhum wa la tukhatibnee fi allatheena zalamoo innahum mughraqoon,” and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the building and the boarding, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the building is the obedience, and the boarding is the salvation, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the instruction, and the building is the obedience, and the verse is the record of the command, the instruction that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own obedience. The believer is taught that the command of the Lord is the guidance, and the one who obeys is the one whose obedience the Lord values, and the believer who follows the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord accepts, and the command to Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the obedience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose obedience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the obedience of the command: Noah built and was saved, and the believer who obeys the command is the believer whose obedience the Lord values, and the command is the teaching that the obedience is the path of the salvation. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the obedience that the command requires.",
  "28": "“Then when you and those with you have settled in the Ark, say, “All praise is for Allah, Who saved us from the wrongdoing people.”” records the praise, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the praise: “Then when you and those with you have settled in the Ark, say, ‘All praise is for Allah, Who saved us from the wrongdoing people,’” or “fa-itha istawayta anta wa man ma‘aka ‘ala al-fulki fa-quli al-hamdu lillahi allathee najjana mina al-qawmi az-zalimeen,” and the commentators note the praise, for the Lord taught Noah the praise, and the praise is the gratitude, the declaration of the thanks, and the verse is the record of the praise, the gratitude that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the praise is the gratitude, and the verse is the record of the praise, the gratitude of the saved. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the praise is the gratitude, and the salvation is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the praise, the gratitude that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the praise, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the praise of the Lord is the gratitude, and the one who is saved is the one whose praise the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the Lord is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the praise of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the gratitude is the thing that the believer must offer, and the believer who reads the praise is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gratitude of the saved: Noah praised and was blessed, and the believer who thanks the Lord is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the praise is the teaching that the gratitude is the response of the saved. The verse is the record of the praise, and the teaching of the gratitude that the salvation requires.",
  "29": "“And pray, “My Lord! Allow me a blessed landing, for You are the best accommodator.”” completes the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the prayer: “And pray, ‘My Lord! Allow me a blessed landing, for You are the best accommodator,’” or “wa qul rabbi anzilnee munzalan mubarakan wa anta khayru al-munzileen,” and the commentators note the completion, for Noah asked the blessed landing, and the completion is the prayer, the seeking of the blessing, and the verse is the record of the completion, the prayer that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the blessed landing is the mercy, and the praise is the trust, and the verse is the record of the completion, the prayer of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the prayer, and the blessing is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the prayer that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer of the believer is the thing that the Lord answers, and the one who seeks the blessing is the one whose blessing the Lord grants, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the prayer of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the blessing is the thing that the believer must seek, and the believer who reads the prayer is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the blessing: Noah sought the blessed landing, and the believer who seeks the blessing is the believer whose blessing the Lord grants, and the prayer is the teaching that the blessing is the thing that the believer must seek. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the prayer carries.",
  "30": "“Surely in this are lessons. And We ˹always˺ put ˹people˺ to the test.”” records the lesson, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the lesson: “Surely in this are lessons. And We always put people to the test,” or “inna fee thalika la-ayatin wa in kunna la-mubtaleen,” and the commentators note the lesson, for the story of Noah is the sign, and the verse is the record of the lesson, the teaching that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the lesson is the sign, and the test is the law, and the verse is the record of the lesson, the teaching of the story. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the lesson is the sign, and the test is the law, and the verse is the record of the lesson, the teaching that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the test, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the test of the Lord is the law, and the one who is tested is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who endures the test is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the lesson of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the test is the thing that the Lord always brings, and the believer who reads the lesson is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the law of the test: the stories are the signs, and the believer who endures the test is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the lesson is the teaching that the test is the thing that the believer must expect. The verse is the record of the lesson, and the teaching of the patience that the test requires.",
  "31": "“Then We raised another generation after them,” records the new generation, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the generation: “Then We raised another generation after them,” or “thumma ansha’na min ba‘dihim qarnan akhareen,” and the commentators note the generation, for the Lord raised the new generation, and the verse is the record of the generation, the step that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the generation is the successor, and the verse is the record of the generation, the step that the following verse develops. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the generation is the successor, and the raising is the creation, and the verse is the record of the generation, the step that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the generation, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the generations succeed one another, and the one who reflects is the one whose reflection the Lord values, and the believer who reads the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the generation of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the history repeats, and the believer who reads the generation is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the succession of the generations: the new generation came and repeated the denial, and the believer who reflects upon the history is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the generation is the teaching that the pattern repeats. The verse is the record of the generation, and the teaching of the reflection that the history invites.",
  "32": "“and sent to them a messenger from among themselves, ˹declaring,˺ “Worship Allah ˹alone˺. You have no god other than Him. Will you not then fear ˹Him˺?”” records the messenger, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the messenger: “and sent to them a messenger from among themselves, declaring, ‘Worship Allah alone. You have no god other than Him. Will you not then fear Him?’” or “fa-arsalna feehim rasoolan minhum ani u‘budoo Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruhu afala tattaqoon,” and the commentators note the messenger, for the Lord sent the messenger, and the verse is the record of the messenger, the call that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the call is the oneness, and the verse is the record of the messenger, the call of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the messenger is the sent, and the call is the oneness, and the verse is the record of the messenger, the call that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the call, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own worship. The believer is taught that the call of the oneness is the message, and the one who worships the One is the one whose worship the Lord values, and the believer who fears the Lord is the believer whose fear the Lord accepts, and the call of the messenger is the teaching, the declaration that the oneness is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the call is the believer whose worship the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the call: the messenger called and was denied, and the believer who worships the One is the believer whose worship the Lord values, and the call is the teaching that the oneness is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the messenger, and the teaching of the worship that the oneness requires.",
  "33": "“But the chiefs of his people—who disbelieved, denied the meeting ˹with Allah˺ in the Hereafter, and were spoiled by the worldly luxuries We had provided for them—said ˹to the masses˺, “This is only a human like you. He eats what you eat, and drinks what you drink.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “But the chiefs of his people—who disbelieved, denied the meeting with Allah in the Hereafter, and were spoiled by the worldly luxuries We had provided for them—said to the masses, ‘This is only a human like you. He eats what you eat, and drinks what you drink,’” or “wa qala al-mala’u min qawmihi allatheena kafaroo wa kaththaboo bi-liqa’i al-akhirati wa atrafnahum fi al-hayati ad-dunya ma hatha illa basharun mithlukum ya’kulu mimma ta’kuloona minhu wa yashrabu mimma tashraboon,” and the commentators note the reply, for the chiefs accused the messenger, and the reply is the rejection, the objection of the humanity, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the three descriptions are the disbelief and the denial and the luxury, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the rejection, and the objection is the humanity, and the verse is the record of the reply, the rejection that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the rejection of the truth is the pattern, and the one who is rejected is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the accusation is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the reply of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the rejection is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the pattern of the rejection: the messenger was accused and denied, and the believer who bears the accusation is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the rejection is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the patience that the rejection requires.",
  "34": "“And if you ˹ever˺ obey a human like yourselves, then you would certainly be losers.”” completes the reply, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse completes the reply: “And if you ever obey a human like yourselves, then you would certainly be losers,” or “wa la-in ata‘tum basharan mithlakum innakum ithan la-khasiroon,” and the commentators note the completion, for the chiefs warned the obedience, and the completion is the rejection, the threat of the loss, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rejection that the following verse continues, and the commentators note the significance, for the warning is the rejection, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rejection of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the rejection, and the warning is the threat, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rejection that the following verse continues.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rejection, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the warning of the falsehood is the rejection, and the one who is warned is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the warning is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the warning: the chiefs warned and were destroyed, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the completion is the teaching that the warning is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the warning cannot shake.",
  "35": "“Does he promise you that once you are dead and reduced to dust and bones, you will be brought forth ˹alive˺?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the question: “Does he promise you that once you are dead and reduced to dust and bones, you will be brought forth alive?” or “a-ya‘idukum annakum itha mittum wa kuntum turaban wa ‘izaman annakum mukhrajoon,” and the commentators note the question, for the chiefs questioned the resurrection, and the question is the denial, the rejection of the promise, and the verse is the record of the question, the denial that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the denial, and the verse is the record of the question, the denial of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the denial, and the resurrection is the promise, and the verse is the record of the question, the denial that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the denial, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the denial of the resurrection is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who denies is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the question of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the denial: the chiefs denied and were destroyed, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the question is the teaching that the denial is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the certainty that the denial cannot shake.",
  "36": "“Impossible, simply impossible is what you are promised!”” records the exclamation, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the exclamation: “Impossible, simply impossible is what you are promised!” or “hayhata hayhata lima too‘adoon,” and the commentators note the exclamation, for the chiefs declared the impossibility, and the exclamation is the denial, the rejection of the promise, and the verse is the record of the exclamation, the denial that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the exclamation is the denial, and the verse is the record of the exclamation, the denial of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the exclamation is the denial, and the impossibility is the rejection, and the verse is the record of the exclamation, the denial that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the denial, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the denial of the promise is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who denies is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the exclamation of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the exclamation is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the constancy of the denial: the chiefs denied and were destroyed, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the exclamation is the teaching that the denial is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the exclamation, and the teaching of the certainty that the denial cannot shake.",
  "37": "“There is nothing beyond our worldly life. We die, others are born, and none will be resurrected.”” records the creed, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the creed: “There is nothing beyond our worldly life. We die, others are born, and none will be resurrected,” or “in hiya illa hayatuna ad-dunya namootu wa nahya wa ma nahnu bi-mab‘ootheen,” and the commentators note the creed, for the chiefs declared the denial of the Hereafter, and the creed is the falsehood, the statement of the material, and the verse is the record of the creed, the falsehood that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the creed is the denial, and the verse is the record of the creed, the falsehood of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the creed is the denial, and the statement is the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the creed, the falsehood that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the creed, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the denial of the Hereafter is the falsehood, and the one who denies is the one whose denial the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the creed of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the thing that the falsehood holds, and the believer who reads the creed is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the falsehood of the denial: the chiefs denied and were destroyed, and the believer who holds the certainty is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the creed is the teaching that the denial is the falsehood. The verse is the record of the creed, and the teaching of the certainty that the denial cannot shake.",
  "38": "“He is no more than a man who has fabricated a lie about Allah, and we will never believe in him.”” completes the creed, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the creed: “He is no more than a man who has fabricated a lie about Allah, and we will never believe in him,” or “in huwa illa rajulun iftara ‘ala Allahi kathiban wa ma nahnu lahu bi-mu’mineen,” and the commentators note the completion, for the chiefs accused the messenger of the fabrication, and the completion is the rejection, the label of the lie, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rejection that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the label is the accusation, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rejection of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the rejection, and the label is the accusation, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rejection that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the label, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the label of the lie is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the one who is labelled is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the completion of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the label is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the label: the messenger was labelled and denied, and the believer who bears the label is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the label is the thing that the truth always faces. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the patience that the label requires.",
  "39": "“The messenger prayed, “My Lord! Help me, because they have denied ˹me˺.”” records the prayer, and the commentators note the two parts of the prayer. The verse records the prayer: “My Lord! Help me, because they have denied me,” or “qala rabbi ansurnee bima kaththaboon,” and the commentators note the prayer, for the messenger sought the help, and the prayer is the recourse, the turning of the messenger to the Lord, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the recourse that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the reliance, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the recourse of the messenger. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prayer is the recourse, and the help is the request, and the verse is the record of the prayer, the recourse that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reliance. The believer is taught that the prayer of the denied is the recourse, and the one who seeks the help is the one whose help the Lord grants, and the believer who relies upon the Lord is the believer whose reliance the Lord values, and the prayer of the messenger is the teaching, the declaration that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the prayer is the believer whose reliance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the recourse of the denied: the messenger prayed and was helped, and the believer who seeks the help is the believer whose help the Lord grants, and the prayer is the teaching that the reliance is the thing that the believer must hold. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the reliance that the denial requires.",
  "40": "“Allah responded, “Soon they will be truly regretful.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two elements of the verse. The verse records the answer: “Soon they will be truly regretful,” or “qala ‘amma qaleelin la-yusbihunna nadimeen,” and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord promised the regret, and the answer is the warning, the declaration of the near punishment, and the verse is the record of the answer, the warning that the following verse fulfills, and the commentators note the significance, for the answer is the warning, and the verse is the record of the answer, the warning of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the answer is the warning, and the regret is the end, and the verse is the record of the answer, the warning that the following verse fulfills.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the answer of the Lord is the warning, and the one who denies is the one whose regret the Lord brings, and the believer who trusts the warning is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the answer of the Lord is the teaching, the declaration that the warning is the thing that the Lord fulfills, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the certainty of the warning: the Lord promised the regret, and the believer who trusts the warning is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the answer is the teaching that the warning is the thing that the Lord fulfills. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the certainty that the warning produces.",
  "41": "“Then the ˹mighty˺ blast overtook them with justice, and We reduced them to rubble. So away with the wrongdoing people!”” records the punishment, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the punishment: “Then the mighty blast overtook them with justice, and We reduced them to rubble. So away with the wrongdoing people!” or “fa-akhathathumu as-sayhatu bil-haqqi fa-ja‘alnahum ghutha’an fa-bu‘dan lil-qawmi az-zalimeen,” and the commentators note the punishment, for the blast overtook the deniers, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the blast is the judgment, and the rubble is the ruin, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the punishment is the judgment, and the rubble is the ruin, and the verse is the record of the punishment, the judgment that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the punishment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own fear. The believer is taught that the punishment of the denial is the judgment, and the one who denies is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the punishment of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the denial is the path of the ruin, and the believer who reads the punishment is the believer whose fear the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the denial: the deniers were destroyed, and the believer who fears the judgment is the believer whose fear the Lord values, and the punishment is the teaching that the denial is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the punishment, and the teaching of the fear that the judgment produces.",
  "42": "\"Then We raised other generations after them\" continues the pattern of the chapter, and the verse is the chapter's second raising, and the second raising is the repetition of the first, and the repetition is the chapter's teaching: the destruction of one people is followed by the raising of another, and the test continues, and the verse says the generations were raised, and the word \"generations\" is plural, and the plural is the chapter's compression of the ages, and the compression is the chapter's method: the centuries between the second generation and Moses are passed over in one line, and the line is the chapter's way of saying that the pattern ran through all of them, and the pattern is the denial and the destruction and the raising, and the reader is asked to see the one line as the whole history, and the history is the chapter's warning. The same pattern is stated elsewhere: \"So We destroyed them, one after the other, reducing them to ˹cautionary˺ tales\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the raising in its one line, and the line is the chapter's bridge to the next verse, and the next verse states the law of the pattern, and the law is that no people can advance or delay their doom, and the law is the chapter's answer to the denial's \"soon\", and the answer is that the term is fixed, and the next verse will state it, and the statement will be the chapter's teaching on the fixed term. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the continuity of the test, and the teaching is the raising, and the raising is the chapter's answer to the despair that follows every destruction, and the answer is that the earth is never left without a people, and the people are never left without the test.",
  "43": "\"No people can advance their doom, nor can they delay it\" states the law of the fixed term, and the verse is the chapter's summary of the pattern's meaning, and the summary is the law, and the law is the chapter's answer to the denial's haste. The verse has two halves, and the two halves close the two exits: \"No people can advance their doom,\" and the verse says the term cannot be brought forward, and the half is the answer to the people who dared the punishment to come now, and the answer is that the term is fixed, and the daring changes nothing. The second half: \"nor can they delay it\", and the verse says the term cannot be pushed back, and the half is the answer to the people who trusted the delay as safety, and the answer is that the term is fixed, and the trust changes nothing. The two halves together are the chapter's teaching on the term: the doom of every people is appointed, and the appointment is God's, and the people are inside the appointment, and the verse says the inside is the only position, and the position is the warning and the comfort at once, the warning for the deniers and the comfort for the believers, and the two are the verse's word. The same law is stated elsewhere: \"If Allah were to punish people ˹immediately˺ for their wrongdoing, He would not have left a single living being on earth. But He delays them to an appointed term,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the law in the middle of the stories, and the placement is the chapter's teaching: the law runs through every story, and the stories are the law's illustrations, and the next verse will state the law's application to the messengers, and the application is the succession, and the succession is the chapter's next word.",
  "44": "\"Then We sent Our messengers in succession: whenever a messenger came to his people, they denied him. So We destroyed them, one after the other, reducing them to ˹cautionary˺ tales. So away with the people who refuse to believe!\" states the law's application to the messengers, and the verse is the chapter's compression of the whole history of prophecy, and the compression is the verse's teaching: the messengers came one after another, and the denial met each one, and the destruction followed the denial, and the three movements are the verse's whole content, and the content is the pattern, and the pattern is the warning. The word \"succession\" is the word of the following one after another, and the verse says the messengers followed, and the denial followed, and the destruction followed, and the three followings are the chapter's picture of the ages, and the picture is the verse's answer to the deniers of its own time: the denial of Muhammad is not a new event but the latest repetition of the oldest event, and the repetition has one ending, and the ending is the destruction, and the verse states the ending in its middle: \"So We destroyed them, one after the other, reducing them to ˹cautionary˺ tales\", and the word \"tales\" is the chapter's irony, and the commentators explain the meaning: the destroyed peoples became stories, and the stories are told as warnings, and the verse says the warning is the peoples' only remaining work, and the work is the chapter's own, and the chapter is the telling, and the telling is the mercy. The verse then closes with the verdict: \"So away with the people who refuse to believe!\" and the word \"refuse\" is the chapter's diagnosis, and the diagnosis is that the denial was a refusal, not an inability, and the verse says the refusal is the ground of the removal, and the removal is the chapter's final word on the succession, and the word is the same as the previous verdicts, and the sameness is the chapter's teaching: the end of the refusers does not change, and the next verse will turn to Moses and Aaron, and the turn is the chapter's movement to the greatest of the stories, and the movement is the chapter's design.",
  "45": "“Then We sent Moses and his brother Aaron with Our signs and compelling proof” records the mission, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the mission: “Then We sent Moses and his brother Aaron with Our signs and compelling proof,” or “thumma arsalna Moosa wa akhahu Haroona bi-ayatina wa sultanin mubeen,” and the commentators note the mission, for the Lord sent the two brothers, and the verse is the record of the mission, the sending that the following verse describes, and the commentators note the significance, for the signs are the proofs, and the verse is the record of the mission, the sending of Moses and Aaron. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the mission is the sending, and the signs are the proofs, and the verse is the record of the mission, the sending that the following verse describes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mission, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own duty. The believer is taught that the mission of the Lord is the sending, and the one who is sent is the one whose duty the Lord values, and the believer who carries the truth is the believer whose duty the Lord accepts, and the mission of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the sending is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the mission is the believer whose duty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the duty of the mission: Moses was sent and carried, and the believer who carries the truth is the believer whose duty the Lord values, and the mission is the teaching that the sending is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the mission, and the teaching of the duty that the sending requires.",
  "46": "“to Pharaoh and his chiefs, but they behaved arrogantly and were a tyrannical people.”” records the response, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the response: “to Pharaoh and his chiefs, but they behaved arrogantly and were a tyrannical people,” or “ila Fir‘awna wa mala’ihi fastakbaroo wa kanoo qawman ‘aleen,” and the commentators note the response, for the chiefs behaved arrogantly, and the verse is the record of the response, the rejection that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the arrogance is the rejection, and the verse is the record of the response, the rejection of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the response is the rejection, and the arrogance is the sin, and the verse is the record of the response, the rejection that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the response, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own humility. The believer is taught that the arrogance is the rejection, and the one who is arrogant is the one whose arrogance the Lord weighs, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the response of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the arrogance is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the response is the believer whose humility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the arrogance: the chiefs were arrogant and were destroyed, and the believer who is humble is the believer whose humility the Lord values, and the response is the teaching that the arrogance is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the response, and the teaching of the humility that the rejection requires.",
  "47": "“They argued, “Will we believe in two humans, like ourselves, whose people are slaves to us?”” records the argument, and the commentators note the two parts of the argument. The verse records the argument: “Will we believe in two humans, like ourselves, whose people are slaves to us?” or “fa-qaloo a-nu’minu li-basharayni mithlina wa qawmuhuma lana ‘abidoon,” and the commentators note the argument, for the chiefs objected to the humanity and the slaves, and the argument is the rejection, the two objections, and the verse is the record of the argument, the rejection that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the two objections are the humanity and the status, and the verse is the record of the argument, the rejection of the chiefs. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the argument is the rejection, and the two objections are the charges, and the verse is the record of the argument, the rejection that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the argument, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the objection of the falsehood is the rejection, and the one who is objected is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who bears the objection is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the argument of the chiefs is the teaching, the declaration that the objection is the thing that the truth faces, and the believer who reads the argument is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the pattern of the objection: the chiefs objected and were destroyed, and the believer who bears the objection is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the argument is the teaching that the objection is the thing that the truth faces. The verse is the record of the argument, and the teaching of the patience that the objection requires.",
  "48": "“So they rejected them both, and ˹so˺ were among those destroyed.”” records the result, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the result: “So they rejected them both, and so were among those destroyed,” or “fa-kaththaboohuma fa-kanoo mina al-muhlakeen,” and the commentators note the result, for the chiefs rejected and were destroyed, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rejection is the cause, and the destruction is the end, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the result is the judgment, and the destruction is the end, and the verse is the record of the result, the judgment that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the result, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the rejection of the truth is the cause of the ruin, and the one who rejects is the one whose end the Lord declares, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the result of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the rejection is the path of the ruin, and the believer who reads the result is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the end of the rejection: the chiefs rejected and were destroyed, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose certainty the Lord confirms, and the result is the teaching that the rejection is the path of the ruin. The verse is the record of the result, and the teaching of the certainty that the judgment produces.",
  "49": "“And We certainly gave Moses the Scripture, so perhaps his people would be ˹rightly˺ guided.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: “And We certainly gave Moses the Scripture, so perhaps his people would be rightly guided,” or “wa laqad atayna Moosa al-kitaba la‘allahum yahtadoon,” and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord gave the Scripture, and the verse is the record of the completion, the gift that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the Scripture is the gift, and the guidance is the purpose, and the verse is the record of the completion, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the completion is the gift, and the guidance is the purpose, and the verse is the record of the completion, the gift that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the gift, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own guidance. The believer is taught that the Scripture is the gift, and the one who is given is the one whose guidance the Lord values, and the believer who follows the guidance is the believer whose guidance the Lord accepts, and the gift of Moses is the teaching, the declaration that the guidance is the thing that the Scripture grants, and the believer who reads the gift is the believer whose guidance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gift of the Scripture: Moses was given the Book, and the believer who follows the guidance is the believer whose guidance the Lord values, and the gift is the teaching that the Scripture is the path of the guidance. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the guidance that the Scripture grants.",
  "50": "\"And We made the son of Mary and his mother a sign, and gave them refuge on high ground—a ˹suitable˺ place for rest with flowing water\" continues the succession of the stories, and the verse turns to Mary and her son, and the turn is the chapter's movement from Moses to Jesus, and the movement is the succession's completion, and the completion is the verse's place in the chapter. The verse has two parts, and the two are the sign and the refuge. The first is the sign: \"And We made the son of Mary and his mother a sign,\" and the word \"sign\" is the chapter's word for the miracles, and the verse says Mary and her son together were made a sign, and the commentators explain the meaning: the birth of Jesus without a father, and the purity of his mother, are the sign for the world, and the verse names both, and the naming is the chapter's honour of the mother and the son, and the honour is the verse's answer to every distortion of their story, and the answer is the sign, and the sign is the chapter's word. The second is the refuge: \"and gave them refuge on high ground—a ˹suitable˺ place for rest with flowing water\", and the verse describes the place of their shelter, and the commentators explain the meaning: the high ground with the flowing water was the place where they were settled in safety, and the verse describes it as a suitable place for rest, and the description is the chapter's tenderness, and the tenderness is the verse's teaching: the mother and the child were sheltered, and the shelter is the sign's companion, and the two together are the chapter's picture of the care that surrounds the sign. The same sign is described elsewhere: \"And ˹remember˺ the one who guarded her chastity, so We breathed into her through Our angel, ˹Gabriel,˺ making her and her son a sign for all peoples,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the sign in the succession, and the keeping is the chapter's method, and the method is the succession, and the succession is the one message, and the next verse will address the messengers together, and the address is the chapter's creed.",
  "51": "\"O messengers! Eat from what is good and lawful, and act righteously. Indeed, I fully know what you do\" addresses all the messengers together, and the verse is the chapter's gathering of the succession, and the gathering is the verse's purpose: the messengers of every story are addressed as one company, and the address is the chapter's teaching that the message was one, and the company is one, and the verse speaks to them all in the plural, and the plural is the chapter's creed. The address has three parts, and the three are the common instruction of the messengers. The first: \"Eat from what is good and lawful,\" and the verse commands the messengers to the lawful eating, and the command is the chapter's answer to the objection of the deniers, who pointed to the messengers' eating as the flaw, and the verse turns the flaw into the instruction: the messengers eat, and their eating is from the good and the lawful, and the eating is the worship, and the verse says the worship is the eating, and the saying is the chapter's teaching on the ordinary life of the messenger. The second: \"and act righteously\", and the verse commands the messengers to the righteous deed, and the command is the chapter's word on the messenger's work, and the work is the deed, and the deed is the message, and the verse says the message is the deed, and the saying is the chapter's teaching on the unity of the word and the act. The third: \"Indeed, I fully know what you do\", and the verse closes with the knowledge, and the closing is the chapter's frame of the address: the messengers are seen, and the seeing is the knowledge, and the knowledge is the accountability, and the verse says the messengers themselves are under the seeing, and the saying is the chapter's teaching on the scope of the knowledge, and the scope is everyone, and the next verse will state the oneness of the messengers' religion, and the statement is the chapter's creed.",
  "52": "\"Surely this religion of yours is ˹only˺ one, and I am your Lord, so fear Me ˹alone˺\" states the creed of the chapter, and the verse is the chapter's summary of everything the stories have shown, and the summary is the oneness. The verse has two parts, and the two are the creed's complete form. The first: \"Surely this religion of yours is ˹only˺ one,\" and the word \"surely\" gives the statement the force of the fixed, and the word \"one\" is the creed's whole content, and the verse says the religion of all the messengers is one, and the oneness is the chapter's answer to every division, and the answer is the succession's meaning, and the meaning is that the messages did not differ, and the differing is the people's work, and the verse states the oneness before the division, and the order is the chapter's teaching: the religion was one before the sects, and the one is the truth, and the sects are the departure. The second: \"and I am your Lord, so fear Me ˹alone˺\", and the verse states the creed's consequence, and the consequence is the fear, and the word \"alone\" closes the creed, and the verse says the fear is of the one Lord, and the fear of the one is the worship of the one, and the worship is the religion, and the verse has stated the religion in its shortest form, and the form is the chapter's creed, and the creed is the same as the calls of the stories, and the sameness is the chapter's point. The same creed is stated elsewhere: \"˹O prophets!˺ Indeed, this religion of yours is ˹only˺ one, and I am your Lord, so worship Me ˹alone˺\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the creed in the succession's middle, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the creed at the center, and the center is the chapter's heart, and the next verse will state the division, and the division is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "53": "\"Yet the people have divided it into different sects, each rejoicing in what they have\" states the division that followed the oneness, and the verse is the chapter's diagnosis of the religious fragmentation, and the diagnosis has two parts. The first is the act: \"Yet the people have divided it into different sects,\" and the word \"divided\" is 'taqatta'u,' the word of the cutting, and the verse says the people cut the one religion into pieces, and the cutting is the chapter's word on the fragmentation, and the word is the picture: the one rope cut into many cords, and the cords are the sects, and the verse says the cutting was the people's work, and the work is the chapter's answer to the claim that the division is the truth, and the answer is that the division is the departure. The second is the state: \"each rejoicing in what they have\", and the word \"rejoicing\" is 'farihun,' the word of the delight, and the verse says each sect delights in its own piece, and the delight is the chapter's diagnosis of the fragmentation's persistence, and the diagnosis is that the sects are not merely different but satisfied, and the satisfaction is the seal, and the verse says the seal is the rejoicing, and the rejoicing is the chapter's warning: the one who rejoices in his piece of the rope has stopped looking for the whole, and the stopped looking is the sects' disease, and the disease is the verse's last word. The same division is described elsewhere: \"Yet the people have divided it into different sects, each rejoicing in what they have\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the division in its place after the creed, and the placement is the chapter's teaching: the oneness is the truth, and the division is the departure, and the departure is the people's, and the next verse will instruct the Prophet on the response to the division, and the response is the leaving, and the leaving is the chapter's next word.",
  "54": "\"So leave them ˹O Prophet˺ in their heedlessness for a while\" instructs the Prophet on the response to the division, and the verse is the chapter's command of the leaving, and the command has two parts that balance the situation. The first is the leaving: \"So leave them ˹O Prophet˺\", and the word \"leave\" is 'dharhum,' the word of the letting alone, and the verse commands the Prophet to leave the divided ones, and the leaving is the chapter's answer to the division, and the answer is the boundary: the Prophet has delivered, and the divided ones have rejoiced in their pieces, and the verse says the delivering is done, and the leaving is the next step, and the step is the chapter's teaching on the end of the argument, and the end is the leaving, and the leaving is not the abandonment but the entrusting. The second is the limit: \"in their heedlessness for a while\", and the word \"heedlessness\" is 'ghamrah,' the word of the overwhelming, and the commentators explain the meaning: the heedlessness is the flood that has covered them, and the verse says the flood has a term, and the word \"for a while\" is the term, and the term is the chapter's warning folded into the leaving: the leaving is for a while, and the while is the mercy, and the while is running, and the reader is inside the running, and the running is the chapter's last word on the divided. The same command is stated elsewhere: \"So leave them ˹O Prophet˺ in their heedlessness for a while\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the command in its place, and the place is the chapter's transition, and the transition is the turn from the stories to the argument, and the turn is the chapter's design, and the next verse will open the argument with the wealth, and the wealth is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "55": "\"Do they think, since We provide them with wealth and children,\" opens the chapter's final argument, and the verse begins with a question that the next verse will complete, and the beginning is the question's first half, and the half is the setup of the chapter's last teaching. The question is aimed at the deniers, and its content is the provision: \"since We provide them with wealth and children,\" and the verse names the two things the deniers trusted most, the wealth and the children, and the naming is the chapter's diagnosis of the misreading, and the diagnosis is that the provision was read as the approval, and the reading is the chapter's target, and the target is the verse's question, and the question is whether the provision proves the approval, and the answer is the next verse's word, and the word is the hastening of the good, and the hastening is the chapter's answer to the misreading, and the answer is the correction, and the correction is the chapter's last teaching on the provision. The same misreading is described elsewhere: \"If Allah were to hasten evil for people as they wish to hasten good, they would have certainly been doomed\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the question open, and the openness is the chapter's method: the question is asked, and the answer follows, and the following is the chapter's next word, and the word is the chapter's closing argument, and the argument will run to the end of the chapter, and the end is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the account, and the account is the chapter's last word. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the question, and the teaching is the asking, and the asking is the chapter's method at its end, and the method is the question that leads to the answer, and the answer is the next verse's word, and the word is the chapter's closing argument.",
  "56": "\"that We hasten to ˹honour˺ them ˹with˺ all kinds of good? No! They are not aware\" completes the question that the previous verse began, and the completion is the chapter's answer to the misreading of the provision, and the answer is the word \"No\". The verse completes the question: do they think that the hastening of the good to them is the honouring, and then the verse answers its own question with the \"No,\" and the \"No\" is the chapter's correction of the deniers' whole worldview, and the correction is the verse's teaching: the provision is not the honouring, and the wealth and the children are not the approval, and the verse says the deniers read the gift as the verdict, and the reading is the error, and the error is the chapter's target. The verse then states the diagnosis: \"They are not aware\", and the word \"aware\" is 'yash'urun,' the word of the perceiving, and the verse says the deniers do not perceive the truth of their provision, and the perceiving is the missing sense, and the missing sense is the chapter's answer to the question: the provision is a test, and the deniers read it as a prize, and the reading is the unawareness, and the unawareness is the verse's last word on the matter. The same correction is stated elsewhere: \"So let neither their wealth nor children impress you ˹O Prophet˺. Allah only intends to torment them through these things in this worldly life,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the correction in the question's completion, and the keeping is the chapter's design: the question is asked, and the \"No\" is the answer, and the answer is the chapter's teaching on the provision, and the teaching is the test, and the test is the chapter's word.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's correction of the materialist reading, and the correction is the \"No,\" and the \"No\" is the chapter's answer to every age that has measured God's approval by the wealth, and the answer is that the wealth is a test, and the test is the unawareness, and the next verse will turn to the opposite group, the ones who are aware, and the turn is the chapter's contrast, and the contrast is the chapter's method, and the method is the two groups, and the two groups are the chapter's next teaching.",
  "57": "\"Surely those who tremble in awe of their Lord\" opens the chapter's description of the aware, and the verse is the first stroke of the portrait that the chapter has been building toward, and the stroke is the trembling. The word \"tremble\" is 'mushfiqun,' the word of the fear that is mixed with the care, and the verse says the aware tremble in awe of their Lord, and the commentators explain the meaning: the trembling is not the terror of the threatened but the awe of the loving, and the awe is the aware ones' first mark, and the verse places the mark at the head of the portrait, and the placement is the chapter's teaching: the awareness begins with the awe, and the awe is the root of everything that follows, and the verse says the awe is of their Lord, and the word \"their\" is the closeness, and the closeness is the awe's ground, and the ground is the chapter's word. The same awe is described elsewhere: \"who were in awe of the Most Compassionate without seeing ˹Him˺, and have come with a heart turning ˹only to Him˺\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the trembling in its place at the head of the list, and the list is the chapter's portrait of the aware, and the portrait is the contrast to the unaware of the previous verse, and the contrast is the chapter's teaching: the two groups are divided by the awe, and the awe is the division, and the next verse will add the second mark, the belief in the revelations, and the list will continue through the marks, and the continuation is the chapter's method. The verse's placement at the head of the list is also the chapter's answer to the question of where the awareness begins, and the answer is the awe, and the awe is the first step of the aware, and the step is the chapter's gift to every reader who wants the success of the opening verse, and the gift is the trembling.",
  "58": "\"and who believe in the revelations of their Lord\" adds the second mark of the aware, and the verse joins the belief to the awe, and the joining is the chapter's teaching: the awe is the heart's state, and the belief is the heart's act, and the two together are the aware ones' foundation, and the verse says the aware believe in the revelations of their Lord, and the word \"revelations\" is 'ayat,' the word of the signs, and the verse says the belief is in the signs, and the signs are the Quran's verses and the world's displays, and the belief in both is the aware ones' second mark, and the verse places the belief after the awe, and the placement is the chapter's order: the awe opens the heart, and the belief fills it, and the opening and the filling are the two movements of the aware heart, and the verse says both, and the saying is the chapter's portrait's second stroke. The same belief is described elsewhere: \"Those We have given the Book follow it as it should be followed. It is they who ˹truly˺ believe in it\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the belief in the list, and the list is the chapter's description of the aware, and the description is the contrast to the deniers of the stories, and the contrast is the chapter's method, and the method is the two groups, and the next verse will add the third mark, the absence of the association, and the absence is the chapter's next stroke. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the belief, and the teaching is the joining, and the joining is the chapter's word on the aware heart, and the word is that the awe and the belief are one state, and the state is the awareness, and the awareness is the chapter's portrait, and the portrait is the contrast to the unaware, and the contrast is the chapter's method.",
  "59": "\"and who associate none with their Lord\" adds the third mark of the aware, and the verse states the mark as an absence, and the absence is the chapter's word on the purity. The verse says the aware associate none with their Lord, and the word \"associate\" is 'yushrikun,' the word of the sharing, and the verse says the sharing is absent, and the absence is the third mark, and the commentators explain the meaning: the aware ones' worship is pure, and the purity is the absence of the partner, and the absence is the chapter's answer to the idolatry of the stories, and the answer is the list's third stroke, and the stroke is the purity, and the purity is the chapter's teaching: the aware heart is the heart that has cleared the space, and the cleared space is the worship, and the worship is the one God's, and the verse says the one God is their Lord, and the word \"their\" is the closeness, and the closeness is the purity's ground, and the ground is the chapter's word. The same purity is described elsewhere: \"Be upright ˹in devotion˺ to Allah, associating none with Him ˹in worship˺\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the absence in the list, and the list is the chapter's portrait of the aware, and the portrait is the contrast to the unaware, and the contrast is the chapter's method, and the method is the two groups, and the next verse will add the fourth mark, the fear in the doing, and the fear is the chapter's next stroke, and the stroke is the portrait's heart. The verse's word 'associate' is also the chapter's link back to its own creed, the creed that God has no partner, and the link is the chapter's teaching that the aware ones' purity is the creed lived, and the living is the third mark, and the mark is the chapter's portrait's stroke.",
  "60": "\"and who do whatever ˹good˺ they do with their hearts fearful, ˹knowing˺ that they will return to their Lord\" adds the fourth mark of the aware, and the verse is the portrait's heart, and the heart is the fear in the doing. The verse says the aware do their good with fearful hearts, and the fear is not the fear of the punishment but the fear of the falling short, and the commentators explain the meaning: the aware ones give their good and tremble that it may not be accepted, and the trembling is the fourth mark, and the mark is the chapter's deepest teaching on the good deed, and the teaching is that the deed is measured by the heart that carries it, and the heart of the aware is the fearful heart, and the fear is the sign that the deed is alive. The verse then states the reason for the fear: \"˹knowing˺ that they will return to their Lord\", and the word \"return\" is 'raj'un,' the word of the going back, and the verse says the aware know the return, and the knowing is the fear's source, and the source is the chapter's teaching: the one who knows the return does his good with the fear, and the fear is the awareness, and the awareness is the portrait's name, and the verse says the name is the return, and the return is the chapter's word. The same fear is described elsewhere: \"There is no blame on those who believe and do good for what they had consumed before ˹the prohibition˺,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the fear in the list, and the list is the chapter's portrait of the aware, and the portrait is the contrast to the unaware, and the contrast is the chapter's method, and the next verse will state the aware ones' racing, and the racing is the portrait's completion.",
  "61": "\"it is they who race to do good deeds, always taking the lead\" completes the portrait of the aware, and the verse states the fifth mark, and the mark is the racing, and the racing is the portrait's completion. The verse says the aware race to the good deeds, and the word \"race\" is 'yusari'un,' the word of the hastening, and the commentators explain the meaning: the aware ones do not merely do the good; they run toward it, and the running is the fifth mark, and the mark is the chapter's teaching on the eager heart, and the teaching is that the aware heart is the eager heart, and the eagerness is the fruit of the fear of the previous verse, and the verse places the racing after the fear, and the placement is the chapter's order: the fear drives the racing, and the racing is the fear's deed, and the two together are the aware ones' complete picture, and the picture is the chapter's portrait, and the portrait is the contrast to the unaware, and the contrast is the chapter's method, and the method is the two groups, and the two groups are the chapter's answer to the question of the provision, and the answer is that the provision is a test, and the test separates the racers from the unaware, and the separation is the chapter's teaching. The same racing is described elsewhere: \"So compete with one another in doing good\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the racing in its place at the portrait's end, and the end is the portrait's completion, and the completion is the chapter's description of the aware, and the description will now be followed by the chapter's word on the record, and the word is the next verse's teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the eagerness, and the teaching is the racing, and the racing is the chapter's word on the good deed, and the word is that the deed is the running, and the running is the aware ones' whole life, and the life is the chapter's portrait, and the portrait is the answer to the unaware, and the answer is the chapter's method.",
  "62": "\"We never require of any soul more than what it can afford. And with Us is a record which speaks the truth. None will be wronged\" follows the portrait of the aware with the chapter's word on the measure, and the verse is the justice of the whole arrangement, and the justice has three parts. The first is the measure: \"We never require of any soul more than what it can afford\", and the word \"afford\" is 'wus',' the word of the capacity, and the verse says the requirement is within the capacity, and the saying is the chapter's answer to the fear that the racing of the previous verse is beyond the human strength, and the answer is that the requirement is measured, and the measure is the mercy, and the mercy is the verse's first word. The second is the record: \"And with Us is a record which speaks the truth\", and the word \"record\" is 'kitab,' the book, and the verse says the book is with God, and the book speaks the truth, and the saying is the chapter's answer to the fear of the forgetting, and the answer is that nothing is forgotten, and the book is the witness, and the witness is the verse's second word. The third is the outcome: \"None will be wronged\", and the word \"wronged\" is 'yuzlamun,' the word of the injustice, and the verse says the wronging is absent, and the saying is the chapter's answer to the fear of the unfairness, and the answer is that the measure and the record guarantee the fairness, and the fairness is the verse's third word, and the three words together are the chapter's justice, and the justice is the frame of the two groups, and the frame is the chapter's teaching on the Day, and the teaching is the next verses' subject. The same measure is stated elsewhere: \"If Allah were to punish people ˹immediately˺ for their wrongdoing, He would not have left a single living being on earth\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the measure in its place, and the place is the bridge to the deniers, and the bridge is the chapter's design.",
  "63": "\"But the hearts of those ˹who disbelieve˺ are oblivious to ˹all of˺ this, and they have other ˹evil˺ deeds, opposite to this, in which they are engrossed\" turns from the aware to the unaware, and the verse is the chapter's diagnosis of the deniers' state, and the diagnosis has two parts. The first is the oblivion: \"the hearts of those ˹who disbelieve˺ are oblivious to ˹all of˺ this,\" and the word \"oblivious\" is 'ghaflah,' the word of the heedless, and the verse says the deniers' hearts are heedless of everything the chapter has described, the provision and the test and the record and the measure, and the heedlessness is the verse's diagnosis of the unaware, and the diagnosis is the chapter's answer to the question of the previous verses: the deniers are not lacking the evidence; they are heedless of it, and the heedlessness is the disease, and the disease is the verse's first word. The second is the engrossment: \"and they have other ˹evil˺ deeds, opposite to this, in which they are engrossed\", and the word \"engrossed\" is 'amilun,' the word of the working, and the verse says the deniers have deeds of their own, and the deeds are the opposite of the aware ones' deeds, and the verse says the deniers are engrossed in their deeds, and the engrossment is the disease's second half, and the half is the chapter's teaching: the heedless heart is not empty; it is full, and the filling is the evil deed, and the deed is the engrossment, and the engrossment is the verse's last word. The same diagnosis is stated elsewhere: \"The life of this world has been made appealing to the disbelievers, and they mock the believers\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the diagnosis in its place, and the place is the contrast to the aware, and the contrast is the chapter's method, and the next verse will describe the seizure, and the seizure is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "64": "\"But as soon as We seize their elite with torment, they start to cry for help\" describes the seizure, and the verse is the chapter's picture of the deniers' awakening, and the picture has two halves that match the two halves of the previous verse. The first is the seizure: \"But as soon as We seize their elite with torment,\" and the word \"elite\" is 'mutrafihim,' the word of the luxuried, and the verse says the seizure falls on the elite, and the commentators explain the meaning: the punishment begins with the leaders of the denial, the ones whose luxury the earlier verses described, and the beginning is the chapter's precision, and the precision is the teaching: the elite who led the denial are the first to meet the torment, and the meeting is the justice, and the justice is the verse's first word. The second is the crying: \"they start to cry for help\", and the word \"cry\" is 'yaj'arun,' the word of the loud pleading, and the verse says the seized begin to plead, and the pleading is the chapter's picture of the awakening, and the picture is the verse's irony: the ones who were engrossed in their deeds are now pleading for the help, and the pleading is the denial's end, and the end is the verse's second word, and the two words together are the chapter's teaching on the seizure, and the teaching is the transition, and the transition is the next verse's answer, and the answer is the refusal of the help, and the refusal is the chapter's next stroke. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the beginning of the end, and the teaching is the elite, and the elite is the chapter's word on the leadership, and the word is that the leaders of the denial lead in the torment as they led in the denial, and the leading is the verse's warning to every leader, and the warning is the chapter's method.",
  "65": "\"˹They will be told,˺\"\"Do not cry for help today. Surely you will never be saved from Us\" records the answer to the crying, and the verse is the chapter's refusal of the help, and the refusal is the seizure's completion, and the completion is the verse's teaching on the closed door. The verse quotes the words that will be spoken to the crying: \"Do not cry for help today\", and the word \"today\" is the verse's key, and the commentators explain the meaning: the help was available in the days before, and the day of the seizure is not the day of the help, and the verse says the door of the pleading is closed, and the closing is the chapter's answer to the crying, and the answer is the refusal, and the refusal is the justice, and the justice is the verse's first word. The verse then states the reason: \"Surely you will never be saved from Us\", and the word \"never\" is the verse's seal, and the verse says the saving is impossible, and the impossibility is the chapter's teaching on the end, and the teaching is that the torment of the seized has no escape, and the escape was the time before, and the time is gone, and the verse says the gone is the today, and the today is the verse's last word. The same refusal is stated elsewhere: \"There they will be ˹fervently˺ screaming, \"Our Lord! Take us out ˹and send us back˺. We will do good, unlike what we used to do!\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the refusal in the story of the seizure, and the keeping is the chapter's method, and the method is the warning, and the warning is the chapter's teaching on the closed door, and the next verses will explain why the door closed, and the explanation is the recitation of the verses, and the recitation is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "66": "\"Indeed, My revelations were recited to you, but you used to back away ˹in disgust˺\" opens the chapter's explanation of the closed door, and the verse is the recitation of the record, and the record is the chapter's answer to the crying of the previous verse, and the answer is the reminder of what was offered. The verse has two halves, and the two are the offer and the refusal. The first is the offer: \"Indeed, My revelations were recited to you,\" and the word \"recited\" is 'tutla,' the word of the reading, and the verse says the revelations were read to them, and the reading is the chapter's record of the mercy, and the mercy is the verse's first word, and the word is the answer to the crying: the help was offered, and the offering was the recitation, and the recitation was the door. The second is the refusal: \"but you used to back away ˹in disgust˺,\" and the word \"back away\" is 'tankisun,' the word of the turning back on the heels, and the verse says the refusal was the backing away, and the commentators explain the word's picture: the deniers turned on their heels at the recitation, and the turning is the verse's record of the refusal, and the record is the chapter's teaching on the closed door, and the teaching is that the door was closed by the backing, and the backing is the verse's last word. The same record is stated elsewhere: \"There are some of them who ˹pretend to˺ listen to your recitation ˹of the Quran˺, but We have cast veils over their hearts\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the record in the explanation, and the keeping is the chapter's method, and the method is the reminder, and the reminder is the chapter's next verses' work, and the work is the list of the refusals, and the list is the chapter's diagnosis of the denial, and the diagnosis is the chapter's teaching.",
  "67": "\"boasting of the Sacred House, and babbling ˹nonsense about the Quran˺ by night\" continues the record of the refusal, and the verse names the two deeds that accompanied the backing away, and the two are the chapter's picture of the denial's life. The first is the boasting: \"boasting of the Sacred House,\" and the word \"boasting\" is 'samiran,' the word of the night talkers, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers of Mecca sat by night around the Sacred House and boasted of their status as its keepers, and the boasting is the verse's record of the pride, and the pride is the chapter's diagnosis of the denial's root, and the root is the claim of the House, and the claim is the verse's irony: the ones who boasted of the House were the ones who barred the believers from it, and the barring is the pride's deed, and the deed is the verse's first word. The second is the babbling: \"and babbling ˹nonsense about the Quran˺ by night\", and the word \"babbling\" is 'tahjurun,' the word of the absurd talk, and the verse says the deniers' night talk was the nonsense about the Quran, and the nonsense is the verse's record of the mockery, and the mockery is the chapter's diagnosis of the denial's manner, and the manner is the verse's second word, and the two words together are the chapter's picture of the denial's night, and the picture is the contrast to the recitation, and the contrast is the chapter's teaching: the same House that should have heard the recitation heard the boasting, and the same night that should have carried the remembrance carried the babbling, and the two replacements are the denial's whole story, and the story is the verse's record, and the record is the chapter's warning.",
  "68": "\"Is it because they have never contemplated the Word ˹of Allah˺? Or ˹because˺ there has come to them something that did not come to their forefathers?\" opens the chapter's questions to the deniers, and the verse is the first of a series of questions, and the series is the chapter's cross-examination of the denial, and the cross-examination is the verse's method: the denial is given its possible reasons, and each reason is shown to be empty. The verse offers two reasons, and the two are the first pair. The first: \"Is it because they have never contemplated the Word ˹of Allah˺?\" and the word \"contemplated\" is 'yaddabbaru,' the word of the deep reflection, and the verse asks whether the denial is the failure of the reflection, and the question is the chapter's diagnosis of the first possibility, and the possibility is the neglect, and the neglect is the verse's first word. The second: \"Or ˹because˺ there has come to them something that did not come to their forefathers?\" and the verse asks whether the denial is the newness, and the question is the chapter's diagnosis of the second possibility, and the possibility is the strangeness, and the verse lets the question stand, and the standing is the chapter's irony: the newness of the message is its proof, not its flaw, and the flaw is the refusal, and the refusal is the verse's second word, and the two questions together are the chapter's opening of the cross-examination, and the opening is the chapter's method, and the method is the questions, and the questions are the next verses' work, and the work is the chapter's answer to the denial. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the questions, and the teaching is the method, and the method is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is that the denial has no reason, and the questions show the reasons' emptiness, and the emptiness is the chapter's word on the denial, and the word is the cross-examination, and the cross-examination is the chapter's next verses' work.",
  "69": "\"Or ˹because˺ they failed to recognize their Messenger, and so they denied him?\" adds the third question, and the verse is the chapter's diagnosis of the denial's most common reason, and the reason is the failure of the recognition, and the verse's question is the chapter's answer to the deniers' own claims. The verse asks whether the denial is the failure to recognize the Messenger, and the word \"failed\" is the word of the not knowing, and the question is the chapter's irony, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Messenger was known to them from his birth, and his truthfulness was their own name for him, and the verse asks whether the recognition failed after the message, and the question is the answer, and the answer is that the recognition did not fail; the acceptance failed, and the failure is the verse's word. The verse then states the consequence: \"and so they denied him\", and the word \"so\" ties the denial to the failed recognition, and the verse says the denial followed the failure, and the following is the chapter's teaching on the denial's logic, and the logic is the circular, and the circle is the verse's diagnosis: the deniers denied the Messenger because they failed to recognize him, and they failed to recognize him because they denied him, and the circle is the denial's whole case, and the case is the verse's exposure, and the exposure is the chapter's method, and the method is the questions, and the next question will name the accusation of madness, and the naming is the chapter's next stroke. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the recognition, and the teaching is the circle, and the circle is the chapter's word on the denial's logic, and the word is that the denial feeds itself, and the feeding is the verse's exposure, and the exposure is the chapter's method, and the method is the questions, and the questions are the chapter's answer.",
  "70": "\"Or ˹because˺ they say,\"\"He is insane?\" In fact, he has come to them with the truth, but most of them are resentful of the truth adds the fourth question, and the verse records the accusation of madness, and the recording is the chapter's answer to the accusation, and the answer is the verse's two halves. The first half records the accusation: \"Or ˹because˺ they say, \"He is insane?\"\" and the verse places the accusation among the reasons of the denial, and the placement is the chapter's irony, and the irony is the teaching: the accusation of madness is not a reason but a dismissal, and the dismissal is the denial's last resort, and the verse records it among the reasons so that the reader sees the resort as the resort, and the seeing is the verse's first word. The second half answers the accusation: \"In fact, he has come to them with the truth, but most of them are resentful of the truth\", and the word \"in fact\" is the verse's correction, and the correction is the chapter's answer: the Messenger came with the truth, and the truth is the opposite of the madness, and the verse then names the real reason: \"most of them are resentful of the truth\", and the word \"resentful\" is 'karihun,' the word of the disliking, and the verse says the denial is the dislike of the truth, and the dislike is the chapter's final diagnosis, and the diagnosis is the verse's last word, and the word is the answer to the whole cross-examination: the denial is not the reflection, not the newness, not the failed recognition, and not the madness; it is the dislike, and the dislike is the chapter's teaching on the denial's root, and the root is the heart's aversion, and the aversion is the next verses' subject.",
  "71": "\"Had the truth followed their desires, the heavens, the earth, and all those in them would have certainly been corrupted\" states the chapter's argument against the desires, and the verse is the answer to the diagnosis of the previous verse, and the answer is the absurdity of the alternative. The verse imagines the alternative: if the truth had followed their desires, and the word \"desires\" is 'ahwa',' the word of the whims, and the verse says the following of the whims would have corrupted everything, and the corruption is the verse's proof, and the proof is the chapter's argument: the truth cannot follow the whims, because the whims are many and conflicting, and the following would tear the heavens and the earth apart, and the verse says the tearing would be certain, and the word \"certainly\" is the verse's seal, and the seal is the chapter's teaching on the truth's independence, and the teaching is that the truth is the order, and the order is the truth's proof, and the proof is the verse's first word. The verse then states the actual gift: \"In fact, We have brought them ˹the means to˺ their glory, but they turn away from it\", and the word \"glory\" is 'dhikrahum,' the word of the remembrance and the honour, and the verse says the truth that came to them is their own glory, and the glory is the chapter's answer to the resentment, and the answer is the irony: they resented the thing that was their honour, and the verse says they turn away from it, and the turning is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the denial's loss, and the loss is the glory, and the next verses will ask about the Prophet's asking, and the asking is the chapter's next question, and the question is the cross-examination's continuation.",
  "72": "\"Or ˹is it because˺ you ˹O Prophet˺ are asking them for tribute? But the reward of your Lord is best, for He is the Best Provider\" adds the fifth question, and the verse addresses the Prophet in the question, and the addressing is the chapter's way of clearing the Messenger of every possible motive, and the clearing is the cross-examination's purpose. The verse asks whether the denial is because the Prophet asks them for a payment, and the word \"tribute\" is 'kharjan,' the word of the tax, and the question is the chapter's answer to the accusation of the profit, and the answer is the question itself, and the question is the irony: the Prophet asked for nothing, and the verse says the asking is not his, and the saying is the chapter's clearing of the Messenger, and the clearing is the verse's first word. The verse then states the Prophet's actual reward: \"But the reward of your Lord is best, for He is the Best Provider\", and the word \"reward\" is 'ajr,' the word of the wage, and the verse says the Prophet's reward is with his Lord, and the reward is the best, and the verse closes with the name: \"He is the Best Provider\", and the name is the chapter's answer to the accusation of the profit, and the answer is that the Provider is God, and the Prophet's provision is from the Provider, and the provision is the best, and the verse says the accusation of the asking is the accusation of the one who needs nothing, and the accusation is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the Messenger's detachment, and the teaching is the cross-examination's fifth stroke, and the next verse will state the Prophet's call, and the call is the chapter's next word.",
  "73": "\"And surely you are calling them to the Straight Path\" states the Prophet's actual work, and the verse is the chapter's answer to all the questions of the cross-examination, and the answer is the call. The verse says the Prophet is calling them to the Straight Path, and the word \"calling\" is 'tad'u,' the word of the invitation, and the word \"Straight\" is 'sirat,' the word of the road, and the verse says the call is to the road, and the road is the path of the truth, and the verse places the call at the end of the cross-examination, and the placement is the chapter's method: the questions were the clearing of the obstacles, and the call is the positive truth, and the truth is the verse's word. The word \"surely\" gives the statement the force of the certainty, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Prophet's mission is not the asking or the madness or the newness; it is the calling, and the calling is to the Straight Path, and the path is the one that leads to the Lord, and the verse says the path is straight, and the straightness is the chapter's answer to the crookedness of the denial, and the answer is the verse's teaching: the cross-examination ends where the call begins, and the call is the chapter's word on the Messenger, and the word is the next verses' subject, and the next verse will state the denial's deviation, and the deviation is the chapter's contrast to the straightness, and the contrast is the chapter's method, and the method is the two ways, and the two ways are the chapter's last teaching on the call. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the mission, and the teaching is the call, and the call is the chapter's word on the Messenger's work, and the word is that the work is the invitation, and the invitation is the chapter's answer to the cross-examination, and the answer is the straight path, and the path is the chapter's next word.",
  "74": "\"but those who disbelieve in the Hereafter are certainly deviating from that Path\" completes the contrast of the previous verse, and the verse states the deniers' direction, and the direction is the deviation, and the deviation is the chapter's answer to the call, and the answer is the two ways. The verse says the deniers of the Hereafter are deviating from the Path, and the word \"deviating\" is 'nakibun,' the word of the turning aside, and the verse says the turning is the deniers' state, and the commentators explain the meaning: the denial of the Hereafter is the root of the deviation, and the deviation is the chapter's diagnosis of the deniers' road, and the diagnosis is the verse's teaching: the one who denies the return has no reason to stay on the path, and the path is left, and the leaving is the deviation, and the verse says the deviation is certain, and the certainty is the chapter's word on the deniers' end, and the word is the contrast's completion, and the completion is the chapter's method: the straight and the deviant, the two ways, and the two ways are the verse's whole content, and the content is the chapter's answer to the cross-examination, and the answer is that the denial is not a mystery but a direction, and the direction is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the test of the mercy, and the test is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the two ways, and the teaching is the deviation, and the deviation is the chapter's word on the denial's direction, and the word is that the denier has chosen the side road, and the side road is the verse's warning, and the warning is the chapter's method, and the method is the contrast, and the contrast is the two ways, and the two ways are the chapter's last word on the call.",
  "75": "\"˹Even˺ if We had mercy on them and removed their affliction, they would still persist in their transgression, wandering blindly\" states the chapter's verdict on the deniers, and the verse is the answer to the question of whether the mercy would change them, and the answer is the verse's \"no\". The verse imagines the mercy: \"if We had mercy on them and removed their affliction,\" and the word \"affliction\" is 'durr,' the word of the harm, and the verse imagines the removal of the harm, and the imagining is the chapter's test of the deniers, and the test is the verse's method: would the mercy turn them, and the verse answers, and the answer is the persistence: \"they would still persist in their transgression, wandering blindly\", and the word \"persist\" is 'lajju,' the word of the stubborn continuing, and the verse says the persistence would remain, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers' disease is not the affliction but the transgression, and the removal of the affliction would not remove the transgression, and the verse says the wandering would continue, and the wandering is the blindness, and the blindness is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's verdict on the deniers, and the verdict is that the mercy would not change them, and the saying is the chapter's teaching on the hardened heart, and the teaching is the chapter's transition to the final sections, and the transition is the chapter's design, and the design is the warning, and the warning is the chapter's remaining verses, and the remaining verses are the chapter's end. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the hardened heart, and the teaching is the persistence, and the persistence is the chapter's word on the disease, and the word is that the disease is the transgression, and the transgression is the verse's verdict, and the verdict is the chapter's method, and the method is the warning, and the warning is the chapter's remaining verses.",
  "76": "\"And We have already seized them with torment, but they never humbled themselves to their Lord, nor did they ˹submissively˺ appeal ˹to Him˺\" opens the chapter's account of the seizure in this world, and the verse is the answer to the question the previous verse raised, and the answer is the record of the tried remedies. The verse has two halves, and the two are the remedy and the failure. The first is the remedy: \"And We have already seized them with torment,\" and the word \"seized\" is 'akhadhna,' the word of the taking, and the verse says the torment was already sent, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers of Mecca had been seized with the drought and the famine and the fear, and the seizure was the remedy, and the remedy is the verse's first word. The second is the failure: \"but they never humbled themselves to their Lord, nor did they ˹submissively˺ appeal ˹to Him˺\", and the verse names the two responses that never came, and the two are the humility and the appeal, and the commentators explain the meaning: the seized did not humble themselves to their Lord, and they did not turn to Him in the pleading, and the verse says the remedy failed, and the failure is the chapter's teaching on the hardened heart, and the teaching is the verse's last word. The same remedy is described elsewhere: \"Whenever We sent a prophet to a society, We afflicted its ˹disbelieving˺ people with suffering and adversity, so perhaps they would be humbled\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the failure in the record, and the record is the chapter's answer to the deniers, and the answer is the next verse's warning, and the warning is the severe punishment, and the punishment is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "77": "\"But as soon as We open for them a gate of severe punishment, they will be utterly desperate\" states the next stage of the seizure, and the verse is the chapter's warning of the escalation, and the escalation is the verse's content. The verse imagines the opening: \"as soon as We open for them a gate of severe punishment,\" and the word \"gate\" is 'bab,' the word of the door, and the verse says the punishment has a gate, and the gate is the severe, and the commentators explain the meaning: the earlier seizure was the light, and the gate is the heavy, and the opening of the gate is the verse's warning, and the warning is the chapter's answer to the failure of the previous verse, and the answer is the escalation, and the escalation is the verse's first word. The verse then states the result: \"they will be utterly desperate\", and the word \"desperate\" is 'mublisun,' the word of the despairing, and the verse says the despair will be the state, and the commentators explain the word's root: the despairing is the one whose hope is cut, and the verse says the hope will be cut, and the cutting is the chapter's teaching on the end of the delay, and the teaching is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's transition to the signs, and the transition is the chapter's design: the warning is stated, and the signs are displayed, and the display is the chapter's argument, and the argument is the next verses' work, and the work is the senses, and the senses are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the escalation, and the teaching is the gate, and the gate is the chapter's word on the mercy's end, and the word is that the mercy has a term, and the term is the gate's opening, and the opening is the warning's work, and the work is the chapter's answer to the failure of the remedy, and the answer is the heavy, and the heavy is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "78": "\"He is the One Who created for you hearing, sight, and intellect. ˹Yet˺ you hardly give any thanks\" opens the chapter's display of the signs, and the verse points to the reader's own faculties, and the pointing is the chapter's method at its most personal. The verse names the three gifts: \"hearing, sight, and intellect\", and the word \"intellect\" is 'af'idah,' the word of the hearts, and the commentators explain the meaning: the three are the tools of the knowledge, the hearing that receives and the sight that sees and the heart that understands, and the verse says the three were created for the reader, and the word \"for you\" is the verse's address, and the address is the chapter's closeness, and the closeness is the verse's first word. The verse then states the response: \"˹Yet˺ you hardly give any thanks\", and the word \"hardly\" is 'qalilan,' the word of the little, and the verse says the thanks is little, and the saying is the chapter's diagnosis of the ingratitude, and the diagnosis is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the gifts: the faculties were given, and the thanks was withheld, and the withholding is the disease, and the disease is the next verses' subject. The same gifts are described elsewhere: \"And Allah brought you out of the wombs of your mothers while you knew nothing, and gave you hearing, sight, and intellect, so perhaps you will be grateful,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the gifts in the display, and the display is the chapter's argument, and the argument is the signs, and the signs are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the nearest of the signs, and the teaching is the faculties, and the faculties are the chapter's word on the gratitude, and the word is that the thanks is the response to the gifts, and the response is the chapter's demand, and the demand is the signs' purpose, and the purpose is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "79": "\"And He is the One Who has dispersed you ˹all˺ over the earth, and to Him you will ˹all˺ be gathered\" adds the second sign, and the verse joins the dispersal and the gathering, and the joining is the verse's argument, and the argument is the return. The verse has two halves, and the two are the journey's two ends. The first is the dispersal: \"And He is the One Who has dispersed you ˹all˺ over the earth,\" and the word \"dispersed\" is 'dhara'akum,' the word of the scattering, and the verse says the scattering was God's doing, and the commentators explain the meaning: the human race was spread over the earth from the one beginning, and the spreading is the verse's first sign, and the sign is the reader's own distribution, and the distribution is the verse's first word. The second is the gathering: \"and to Him you will ˹all˺ be gathered\", and the word \"gathered\" is 'tuhsharun,' the word of the assembling, and the verse says the gathering is to God, and the gathering is the verse's second sign, and the sign is the return, and the commentators explain the meaning: the One who scattered the race can gather it, and the gathering is the answer to the deniers of the resurrection, and the answer is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the return, and the teaching is the argument's heart, and the heart is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the life and the death, and the two are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the journey, and the teaching is the two ends, and the two ends are the chapter's word on the return, and the word is that the scattering and the gathering are one power, and the power is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the gathering, and the gathering is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "80": "\"And He is the One Who gives life and causes death, and to Him belongs the alternation of the day and night. Will you not then understand?\" adds the third sign, and the verse states the two great alternations, and the two are the chapter's argument for the return, and the argument is the verse's question at the end. The verse has three parts, and the three are the signs and the question. The first is the life: \"And He is the One Who gives life and causes death,\" and the verse states the two acts of God, and the commentators explain the meaning: the giving of the life and the causing of the death are the two ends of every creature, and the verse says the two ends are God's, and the saying is the verse's first sign. The second is the alternation: \"and to Him belongs the alternation of the day and night\", and the word \"alternation\" is 'ikhtilaf,' the word of the differing, and the verse says the alternation of the day and the night belongs to God, and the commentators explain the meaning: the daily rotation is the same power as the giving and the taking of the life, and the verse places the two signs together, and the placing is the verse's second sign, and the two signs together are the chapter's proof of the power, and the proof is the verse's third part, and the third is the question: \"Will you not then understand?\" and the word \"understand\" is 'ta'qilun,' the word of the reasoning, and the verse asks whether the reasoning has failed, and the question is the chapter's appeal to the mind, and the appeal is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's method: the signs are displayed, and the question is asked, and the answer is the reader's, and the reader's answer is the chapter's teaching. The same signs are described elsewhere: \"How can you deny Allah? You were lifeless and He gave you life, then He will cause you to die and again bring you to life, and then to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the signs in the display, and the display is the chapter's argument, and the argument is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the deniers' old saying, and the saying is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "81": "\"But they ˹just˺ say what their predecessors said\" opens the chapter's quotation of the deniers' saying, and the verse is the bridge from the signs to the denial, and the bridge is the verse's diagnosis: the deniers have no new word, and the saying of the predecessors is their saying, and the sameness is the chapter's teaching on the denial, and the teaching is the verse's whole content. The word \"just\" is the verse's dismissal, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers of Mecca did not reason against the signs; they repeated the words of the earlier deniers, and the repetition is the chapter's diagnosis, and the diagnosis is the verse's first word, and the word is the answer to the signs: the signs were displayed, and the response was the inherited saying, and the saying is the verse's second word, and the two words together are the chapter's teaching on the denial's poverty, and the poverty is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the saying itself, and the saying is quoted in the next verses, and the quotation is the chapter's method, and the method is the record, and the record is the chapter's warning. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the denial's poverty, and the teaching is the repetition, and the repetition is the chapter's word on the inherited saying, and the word is that the saying has no owner, and the ownerless saying is the denial's whole stock, and the stock is the chapter's answer's target, and the target is the next verses' work, and the work is the quotation, and the quotation is the chapter's method. The verse's single line is also the chapter's bridge, and the bridge is the diagnosis, and the diagnosis is the chapter's answer to the signs of the previous verses, and the answer is that the deniers' saying is borrowed, and the borrowed is the chapter's target, and the target is quoted in the next verse.",
  "82": "They said, \"Once we are dead and reduced to dust and bones, will we really be resurrected?\" quotes the inherited saying, and the verse is the first half of the denial's creed, and the half is the question of the impossibility, and the question is the chapter's target, and the target is the verse's content. The verse quotes the deniers' question: \"Once we are dead and reduced to dust and bones, will we really be resurrected?\" and the question is built on the two images of the dissolution, the dust and the bones, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers measured the resurrection against the state of the dead body, and the measurement is the denial's whole argument, and the argument is the verse's record, and the record is the chapter's method, and the method is the quotation, and the quotation is the chapter's answer's setup, and the setup is the verse's work, and the work is the first half of the creed, and the second half is the next verse's word, and the word is the claim of the promise's oldness, and the oldness is the chapter's next teaching. The same question is recorded elsewhere: \"And they say ˹mockingly˺, \"When we are reduced to bones and ashes, will we really be raised as a new creation?\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the question in the deniers' mouths, and the keeping is the chapter's record, and the record is the chapter's answer, and the answer is the signs of the previous verses, and the signs are the chapter's proof, and the proof is the chapter's next word. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the question, and the teaching is the record, and the record is the chapter's word on the denial, and the word is that the denial is a question, and the question is the chapter's answer's setup, and the setup is the next verses' signs, and the signs are the chapter's proof, and the proof is the chapter's remaining work.",
  "83": "\"We have already been promised this, as well as our forefathers earlier. This is nothing but ancient fables!\"\" completes the inherited saying, and the verse is the second half of the denial's creed, and the half is the dismissal, and the dismissal is the chapter's diagnosis of the denial's end. The verse has two parts, and the two are the claim and the verdict. The first is the claim: \"We have already been promised this, as well as our forefathers earlier\", and the verse records the deniers' claim of the promise's oldness, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers said the promise of the resurrection was made to their forefathers as well, and the forefathers died and were not raised, and the claim is the denial's argument from the delay, and the argument is the verse's first record, and the record is the chapter's method. The second is the verdict: \"This is nothing but ancient fables!\" and the word \"fables\" is 'asatir,' the word of the old tales, and the verse records the deniers' verdict on the promise, and the verdict is the dismissal, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers called the promise a tale of the ancients, and the calling is the denial's last word, and the word is the verse's second record, and the two records together are the chapter's picture of the denial's end, and the picture is the chapter's answer's setup, and the setup is the next verse's question, and the question is the chapter's cross-examination, and the cross-examination is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the dismissal, and the teaching is the fables, and the fables are the chapter's word on the denial's verdict, and the word is that the verdict is the denial's last, and the last is the chapter's answer's target, and the target is the cross-examination, and the cross-examination is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "84": "\"Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺,\"\"To whom belong the earth and all those on it, if you ˹really˺ know?\" opens the chapter's cross-examination, and the verse is the first question, and the question is the chapter's method at its sharpest, and the sharpness is the verse's content. The verse instructs the Prophet to ask the deniers the question of the ownership: \"To whom belong the earth and all those on it, if you ˹really˺ know?\" and the question is built on the deniers' own knowledge, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers knew the answer, and the question forces the answer, and the forcing is the chapter's method, and the method is the question that leaves no exit, and the question is the verse's work, and the work is the cross-examination's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the fables of the previous verse, and the answer is the next verse's reply, and the reply is the deniers' own admission, and the admission is the chapter's next teaching. The same question is asked elsewhere: \"Ask ˹them, O Prophet˺, \"Who provides for you from the heavens and the earth?\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the question in the cross-examination, and the keeping is the chapter's method, and the method is the questions, and the questions are the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the series of the admissions, and the series is the chapter's next verses' work. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the question, and the teaching is the method, and the method is the chapter's word on the argument, and the word is that the question is the answer, and the answer is the admission, and the admission is the chapter's next verses' work, and the work is the series of the admissions, and the series is the chapter's cross-examination.",
  "85": "\"They will reply,\"\"To Allah!\" Say, \"Why are you not then mindful?\" records the first admission, and the verse is the cross-examination's first result, and the result is the chapter's point: the deniers know the truth and deny it, and the knowing is the verse's content. The verse quotes the deniers' reply: \"To Allah!\" and the word is the admission, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers could not answer the question of the ownership except with the name of Allah, and the answer is the chapter's record of the knowledge, and the record is the verse's first word, and the word is the cross-examination's result, and the result is the contradiction, and the contradiction is the verse's second word, and the word is the instruction: \"Say, \"Why are you not then mindful?\"\" and the question is the chapter's answer to the admission, and the answer is the call to the mindfulness, and the commentators explain the meaning: the one who knows that the earth belongs to Allah should be mindful of Allah, and the mindfulness is the question's demand, and the demand is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the contradiction, and the teaching is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the series of the questions, and the series is the chapter's cross-examination, and the cross-examination is the chapter's method, and the method is the answer to the denial, and the answer is the chapter's remaining verses. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the contradiction, and the teaching is the admission, and the admission is the chapter's word on the knowledge, and the word is that the deniers know and deny, and the knowing and the denying are the verse's whole content, and the content is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the mindfulness, and the mindfulness is the chapter's demand.",
  "86": "\"˹And˺ ask ˹them˺,\"\"Who is the Lord of the seven heavens and the Lord of the Mighty Throne?\" continues the cross-examination, and the verse is the second question, and the question rises from the earth of the first question to the heavens, and the rising is the chapter's method, and the method is the ascent, and the ascent is the verse's content. The verse instructs the Prophet to ask the question of the heavens: \"Who is the Lord of the seven heavens and the Lord of the Mighty Throne?\" and the question names the two heights, the seven heavens and the Mighty Throne, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers knew the answer to this question as they knew the answer to the first, and the question forces the second admission, and the forcing is the chapter's method, and the method is the question that leaves no exit, and the question is the verse's work, and the work is the cross-examination's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the denial's fables, and the answer is the next verse's admission, and the admission is the chapter's next teaching. The same question is asked elsewhere: \"Or ˹ask them,˺ \"Who created the heavens and the earth, and sends down rain for you from the sky, by which We cause delightful gardens to grow?\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the question in the series, and the keeping is the chapter's method, and the method is the ascent, and the ascent is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the admissions, and the admissions are the chapter's next verses' work. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the ascent, and the teaching is the question, and the question is the chapter's word on the knowledge, and the word is that the knowledge rises with the question, and the rising is the chapter's method, and the method is the cross-examination, and the cross-examination is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the admissions.",
  "87": "\"They will reply,\"\"Allah.\" Say, \"Will you not then fear ˹Him˺?\" records the second admission, and the verse is the cross-examination's second result, and the result is the same as the first, and the sameness is the chapter's point: the deniers' knowledge is complete, and the knowledge is the verse's content. The verse quotes the deniers' reply: \"Allah,\" and the word is the admission, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers answered the question of the heavens with the name of Allah, and the answer is the chapter's record of the knowledge, and the record is the verse's first word, and the word is the cross-examination's result, and the result is the contradiction, and the contradiction is the verse's second word, and the word is the instruction: \"Say, \"Will you not then fear ˹Him˺?\"\" and the question is the chapter's answer to the admission, and the answer is the call to the fear, and the commentators explain the meaning: the one who knows that Allah is the Lord of the seven heavens should fear Him, and the fear is the question's demand, and the demand is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the contradiction, and the teaching is the series' continuation, and the continuation is the next verse's question, and the question is the authority, and the authority is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the fear, and the teaching is the admission, and the admission is the chapter's word on the knowledge, and the word is that the deniers know and deny, and the knowing and the denying are the verse's whole content, and the content is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the fear, and the fear is the chapter's demand, and the demand is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "88": "\"Ask ˹them also,˺\"\"In Whose Hands is the authority over all things, protecting ˹all˺ while none can protect against Him, if you ˹really˺ know?\" continues the cross-examination, and the verse is the third question, and the question moves from the ownership to the authority, and the movement is the chapter's method, and the method is the completion, and the completion is the verse's content. The verse instructs the Prophet to ask the question of the authority: \"In Whose Hands is the authority over all things, protecting ˹all˺ while none can protect against Him, if you ˹really˺ know?\" and the question names the two sides of the authority, and the commentators explain the meaning: the authority is the power of the protection, and the question asks who protects and who cannot be protected against, and the two sides are the question's whole content, and the content is the chapter's method, and the method is the question that leaves no exit, and the question is the verse's work, and the work is the cross-examination's third stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the denial's fables, and the answer is the next verse's admission, and the admission is the chapter's next teaching. The same question is asked elsewhere: \"Or ˹ask them,˺ \"Who guides you in the darkness of the land and sea, and sends the winds ushering in His mercy?\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the question in the series, and the keeping is the chapter's method, and the method is the completion, and the completion is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the admissions, and the admissions are the chapter's next verses' work. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the authority, and the teaching is the question, and the question is the chapter's word on the protection, and the word is that the protection is God's alone, and the aloneness is the chapter's answer to the idols, and the answer is the series' continuation, and the continuation is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "89": "\"They will reply,\"\"Allah.\" Say, \"How are you then so deluded?\" records the third admission, and the verse is the cross-examination's third result, and the result is the same as the two before it, and the sameness is the chapter's point, and the point is the verse's content. The verse quotes the deniers' reply: \"Allah,\" and the word is the admission, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers answered the question of the authority with the name of Allah, and the answer is the chapter's record of the knowledge, and the record is the verse's first word, and the word is the cross-examination's result, and the result is the contradiction, and the contradiction is the verse's second word, and the word is the instruction: \"Say, \"How are you then so deluded?\"\" and the question is the chapter's answer to the admission, and the answer is the word \"deluded,\" and the commentators explain the meaning: the one who knows that Allah holds the authority and still worships the idols is deluded, and the delusion is the question's diagnosis, and the diagnosis is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the contradiction, and the teaching is the series' end, and the end is the next verse's verdict, and the verdict is the liars, and the liars are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the delusion, and the teaching is the admission, and the admission is the chapter's word on the knowledge, and the word is that the deniers know and deny, and the knowing and the denying are the verse's whole content, and the content is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the delusion, and the delusion is the chapter's diagnosis. The verse's word 'deluded' is also the chapter's diagnosis of the contradiction, and the diagnosis is the series' end, and the end is the chapter's answer to the deniers' three admissions, and the answer is that the knowing and the denying cannot share one heart, and the sharing is the delusion.",
  "90": "\"In fact, We have brought them the truth, and they are certainly liars\" states the verdict after the admissions, and the verse is the cross-examination's conclusion, and the conclusion is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the verse's content. The verse has two halves, and the two are the truth and the lie. The first is the truth: \"In fact, We have brought them the truth,\" and the word \"in fact\" is the verse's correction, and the correction is the chapter's word on the message, and the word is that the message is the truth, and the truth was brought, and the bringing is the verse's first word, and the word is the answer to the fables of the earlier verses, and the answer is the chapter's teaching on the message's source, and the source is the truth. The second is the lie: \"and they are certainly liars\", and the word \"liars\" is 'kadhibun,' the word of the falsifiers, and the verse says the deniers are the liars, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers' admissions in the cross-examination proved their knowledge, and the knowledge makes their denial a lie, and the verse says the lie is certain, and the certainty is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's verdict on the denial, and the verdict is the cross-examination's result, and the result is the chapter's teaching on the denial's nature, and the nature is the lie, and the lie is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the oneness, and the oneness is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the verdict, and the teaching is the liars, and the liars are the chapter's word on the denial, and the word is that the denial is a lie, and the lie is the verse's whole content, and the content is the chapter's answer to the fables, and the answer is the truth, and the truth is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "91": "\"Allah has never had ˹any˺ offspring, nor is there any god besides Him. Otherwise, each god would have taken away what he created, and they would have tried to dominate one another. Glorified is Allah above what they claim!\" states the chapter's creed against the lies, and the verse is the famous argument of the mutual exclusion, and the argument is the chapter's answer to every claim of the partner, and the answer is the verse's content. The verse has three parts, and the three are the creed, the proof, and the praise. The first is the creed: \"Allah has never had ˹any˺ offspring, nor is there any god besides Him\", and the verse states the two negations, and the commentators explain the meaning: the negation of the offspring and the negation of the other god are the one creed, and the creed is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the claims, and the answer is the oneness. The second is the proof: \"Otherwise, each god would have taken away what he created, and they would have tried to dominate one another\", and the verse states the argument, and the commentators explain the meaning: if there were two gods, each would take his own creation, and the two would fight for the rule, and the fighting would corrupt the world, and the verse says the corruption is absent, and the absence is the proof, and the proof is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's argument for the oneness, and the argument is the order of the world, and the order is the proof. The third is the praise: \"Glorified is Allah above what they claim!\" and the verse closes with the glorification, and the glorification is the verse's third word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the claims, and the answer is the exaltation, and the exaltation is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the oneness, and the teaching is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the knowledge, and the knowledge is the chapter's next teaching. The same argument is stated elsewhere: \"Had there been other gods besides Allah in the heavens or the earth, both ˹realms˺ would have surely been corrupted\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the argument in its place, and the place is the creed's center, and the center is the chapter's heart.",
  "92": "\"˹He is the˺ Knower of the seen and unseen. Exalted is He above what they associate ˹with Him˺\" completes the creed of the previous verse, and the verse is the chapter's closing of the oneness, and the closing is the two names, and the two names are the verse's content. The verse has two parts, and the two are the knowledge and the exaltation. The first is the knowledge: \"˹He is the˺ Knower of the seen and unseen\", and the word \"Knower\" is 'alim,' the word of the knowing, and the verse says God knows the seen and the unseen, and the commentators explain the meaning: the knowledge covers everything, and the coverage is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the claims of the partner, and the answer is the knowledge, and the knowledge is the oneness's ground, and the ground is the verse's first word. The second is the exaltation: \"Exalted is He above what they associate ˹with Him˺\", and the word \"Exalted\" is 'ta'ala,' the word of the rising above, and the verse says God is above everything they associate with Him, and the commentators explain the meaning: the exaltation is the chapter's answer to the association, and the answer is the rising above, and the rising is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the purity, and the teaching is the oneness's completion, and the completion is the chapter's transition to the Prophet's prayer, and the transition is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the asking, and the asking is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the purity, and the teaching is the exaltation, and the exaltation is the chapter's word on the association, and the word is that God is above the partners, and the aboveness is the verse's whole content, and the content is the chapter's answer to the claims, and the answer is the oneness, and the oneness is the chapter's creed.",
  "93": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺\"\"My Lord! Should You show me what they are threatened with,\" opens the chapter's final prayer, and the verse is the first half of the Prophet's supplication, and the half is the condition, and the condition is the verse's content. The verse instructs the Prophet to pray: \"My Lord! Should You show me what they are threatened with,\" and the prayer is the conditional, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Prophet asks God that if he is shown the punishment that the deniers are threatened with, then the next verse's request would follow, and the condition is the verse's work, and the work is the chapter's teaching on the Prophet's posture, and the teaching is the humility, and the humility is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the deniers' demand for the hastening, and the answer is the prayer, and the prayer is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's transition to the second half, and the second half is the next verse's word, and the word is the request, and the request is the chapter's next teaching. The same posture is described elsewhere: \"Say, \"You will not be accountable for our misdeeds, nor will we be accountable for your deeds,\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the prayer in the chapter's end, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the prayer at the end, and the end is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the account, and the account is the chapter's remaining verses. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the posture, and the teaching is the prayer, and the prayer is the chapter's word on the Prophet, and the word is that the Prophet prays, and the praying is the verse's whole content, and the content is the chapter's answer to the deniers' demand, and the answer is the humility, and the humility is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "94": "\"then, my Lord, do not count me among the wrongdoing people\" completes the prayer, and the verse is the second half of the supplication, and the half is the request, and the request is the chapter's teaching on the Prophet's humility, and the humility is the verse's content. The verse quotes the request: \"then, my Lord, do not count me among the wrongdoing people\", and the word \"count\" is 'taj'alni,' the word of the placing, and the verse says the Prophet asks not to be placed among the wrongdoers, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Prophet, who is the most protected of creation, still prays not to be counted among the wrongdoers, and the prayer is the chapter's teaching on the fear, and the fear is the verse's first word, and the word is the answer to the deniers' arrogance, and the answer is the contrast, and the contrast is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the believer's posture, and the posture is the prayer, and the prayer is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's transition to the assurance, and the assurance is the next verse's word, and the word is the ability, and the ability is the chapter's next teaching. The same prayer is described elsewhere: \"If only you could see the wicked hanging their heads ˹in shame˺ before their Lord,\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the prayer in the chapter's end, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the prayer, and the prayer is the chapter's answer to the deniers, and the answer is the next verse's assurance, and the assurance is the chapter's closing. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the fear, and the teaching is the prayer, and the prayer is the chapter's word on the believer, and the word is that the believer prays against the counting, and the counting is the verse's whole content, and the content is the chapter's answer to the arrogance, and the answer is the contrast, and the contrast is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "95": "\"We are indeed able to show you what We have threatened them with\" states the assurance that follows the prayer, and the verse is the chapter's answer to the Prophet's supplication, and the answer is the ability, and the ability is the verse's content. The verse says: \"We are indeed able to show you what We have threatened them with\", and the word \"indeed\" is the verse's emphasis, and the commentators explain the meaning: the ability to show the punishment is God's, and the showing is not a difficulty for Him, and the verse says the threat is real, and the reality is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the deniers' doubt, and the answer is the certainty, and the certainty is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the promise, and the teaching is the assurance, and the assurance is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's transition to the final sections, and the transition is the chapter's design, and the design is the assurance before the closing, and the closing is the chapter's remaining verses, and the remaining verses are the chapter's end, and the end is the chapter's teaching on the good and the evil, and the teaching is the chapter's final word. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the ability, and the teaching is the assurance, and the assurance is the chapter's word on the threat, and the word is that the threat is real, and the reality is the verse's whole content, and the content is the chapter's answer to the doubt, and the answer is the certainty, and the certainty is the chapter's next teaching. The verse's word 'indeed' is also the chapter's emphasis, and the emphasis is the assurance's strength, and the strength is the chapter's answer to the deniers' demand for the hastening, and the answer is that the showing is God's, and the showing is the chapter's final word on the threat.",
  "96": "\"Respond to evil with what is best. We know well what they claim\" opens the chapter's final section, and the verse is the instruction of the response, and the instruction is the chapter's word on the believer's manner, and the manner is the verse's content. The verse commands the Prophet: \"Respond to evil with what is best\", and the word \"evil\" is 'sayyi'ah,' the word of the bad deed, and the word \"best\" is 'ahsan,' the word of the better, and the verse says the response to the bad is the better, and the commentators explain the meaning: the command is to answer the harm with the good, and the answering is the chapter's teaching on the manner, and the teaching is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the deniers' harm, and the answer is the good, and the good is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's instruction on the response's power, and the power is that the good response breaks the circle of the harm, and the breaking is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's transition to the second half, and the half is the assurance: \"We know well what they claim\", and the word \"know\" is the verse's comfort, and the commentators explain the meaning: the claims of the deniers are known to God, and the knowing is the believer's release, and the release is the verse's final word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the entrusting, and the teaching is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the refuge, and the refuge is the chapter's next teaching. The same command is stated elsewhere: \"Good and evil cannot be equal. Respond ˹to evil˺ with what is best, then the one you are in a feud with will be like a close friend\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the command in the final section, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the manner at the end, and the end is the chapter's destination.",
  "97": "And say, \"My Lord! I seek refuge in You from the temptations of the devils\" opens the chapter's final prayers, and the verse is the first half of the refuge, and the half is the seeking, and the seeking is the verse's content. The verse instructs the Prophet to pray: \"My Lord! I seek refuge in You from the temptations of the devils\", and the word \"temptations\" is 'hamazat,' the word of the poking and the pricking, and the commentators explain the meaning: the temptations of the devils are their whispers, and the whispers are the poking, and the verse says the refuge is sought from them, and the seeking is the chapter's teaching on the protection, and the teaching is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the evil of the previous verse, and the answer is the refuge, and the refuge is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's instruction on the believer's shield, and the shield is the prayer, and the prayer is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's transition to the second half, and the half is the nearness, and the nearness is the next verse's word, and the word is the chapter's next teaching. The same refuge is commanded elsewhere: \"And if you are tempted by Satan, then seek refuge with Allah. Indeed, He ˹alone˺ is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the refuge in the final section, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the prayers at the end, and the end is the chapter's destination. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the shield, and the teaching is the seeking, and the seeking is the chapter's word on the protection, and the word is that the protection is the prayer, and the prayer is the believer's own, and the own is the chapter's answer to the whisper, and the answer is the refuge, and the refuge is the chapter's next word.",
  "98": "\"And I seek refuge in You, my Lord, that they ˹even˺ come near me\" completes the prayer, and the verse is the second half of the refuge, and the half is the nearness, and the nearness is the verse's content. The verse quotes the second seeking: \"And I seek refuge in You, my Lord, that they ˹even˺ come near me\", and the word \"near\" is 'yahdurun,' the word of the attending, and the verse says the Prophet seeks refuge from the devils' very nearness, and the commentators explain the meaning: the prayer asks not only for the protection from the harm but from the approach, and the asking is the chapter's teaching on the prevention, and the teaching is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the whisper, and the answer is the distance, and the distance is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's instruction on the believer's vigilance, and the vigilance is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's transition to the final scenes, and the transition is the chapter's design: the prayers close the instructions, and the scenes open the warning, and the warning is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the death, and the death is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's completion of the refuge, and the completion is the two halves, and the two halves are the chapter's teaching on the shield, and the teaching is the seeking, and the seeking is the believer's own, and the next verses will show the deniers' seeking at the wrong time, and the showing is the chapter's contrast, and the contrast is the chapter's method, and the method is the two seekings, and the two seekings are the chapter's next verses' work.",
  "99": "\"When death approaches any of them, they cry,\"\"My Lord! Let me go back,\" opens the chapter's final scenes, and the verse is the first of the deathbed dialogues, and the dialogue is the chapter's warning, and the warning is the verse's content. The verse describes the moment: \"When death approaches any of them, they cry,\" and the word \"approaches\" is 'hadara,' the word of the arriving, and the verse says the death arrives, and the arrival is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the certainty, and the teaching is that the death arrives for everyone, and the arrival is the deniers' moment of truth, and the moment is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the cry: \"My Lord! Let me go back,\" and the word \"go back\" is 'irji'un,' the word of the returning, and the verse quotes the cry, and the commentators explain the meaning: the denier asks for the return to the world at the moment of the death, and the asking is the verse's picture of the regret, and the regret is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the deathbed, and the teaching is the next verse's answer, and the answer is the \"Never\", and the \"Never\" is the chapter's next teaching. The same cry is recorded elsewhere: \"There they will be ˹fervently˺ screaming, \"Our Lord! Take us out ˹and send us back˺. We will do good, unlike what we used to do!\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the cry in the deathbed, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the scene, and the scene is the chapter's warning, and the warning is the chapter's remaining verses. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the deathbed, and the teaching is the cry, and the cry is the chapter's word on the regret, and the word is that the regret is the death's fruit, and the fruit is the chapter's answer to the heedlessness, and the answer is the next verse's \"Never\", and the \"Never\" is the chapter's final word on the appeal.",
  "100": "\"so I may do good in what I left behind.\"\" Never! It is only a ˹useless˺ appeal they make. And there is a barrier behind them until the Day they are resurrected completes the deathbed dialogue, and the verse is the chapter's answer to the cry, and the answer is the \"Never\", and the \"Never\" is the verse's content. The verse quotes the cry's completion: \"so I may do good in what I left behind\", and the word \"left behind\" is 'taraktu,' the word of the leaving, and the verse says the dying asks to return to do the good in what he left, and the asking is the chapter's picture of the regret, and the regret is the verse's first word, and the word is the answer's setup, and the setup is the \"Never\": \"Never! It is only a ˹useless˺ appeal they make\", and the word \"Never\" is 'kalla,' the word of the strong refusal, and the verse says the appeal is refused, and the commentators explain the meaning: the appeal is the word of the one who has nothing, and the verse calls it the word, and the calling is the chapter's teaching on the deathbed's appeal, and the teaching is that the appeal changes nothing, and the verse then states the reality: \"And there is a barrier behind them until the Day they are resurrected\", and the word \"barrier\" is 'barzakh,' the word of the partition, and the verse says the barrier stands behind them, and the commentators explain the meaning: the time of the return is closed, and the partition holds until the Day, and the holding is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the closed door, and the teaching is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the Trumpet, and the Trumpet is the chapter's next teaching. The same barrier is described elsewhere: \"they are exposed to the Fire ˹in their graves˺ morning and evening. And on the Day the Hour will be established\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the barrier in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the warning, and the warning is the chapter's remaining verses.",
  "101": "\"Then, when the Trumpet will be blown, there will be no kinship between them on that Day, nor will they ˹even care to˺ ask about one another\" opens the chapter's scene of the Trumpet, and the verse is the first stroke of the Day, and the stroke is the severing, and the severing is the verse's content. The verse states the moment: \"Then, when the Trumpet will be blown,\" and the word \"Trumpet\" is 'sur,' the word of the horn, and the verse says the blowing comes, and the coming is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Day's beginning, and the teaching is the blowing, and the blowing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the severing: \"there will be no kinship between them on that Day, nor will they ˹even care to˺ ask about one another\", and the word \"kinship\" is 'ansab,' the word of the lineages, and the verse says the lineages are severed, and the commentators explain the meaning: the bonds of the blood that ruled the world are cut, and the verse says the asking is absent, and the absence is the chapter's picture of the terror, and the picture is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the Day's solitude, and the teaching is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the scales, and the scales are the chapter's next teaching. The same severing is described elsewhere: \"on that Day every person will flee from their own siblings\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the severing in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the Day, and the Day is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the chapter's remaining verses. The verse's mention of the Trumpet is also the chapter's link to its own scenes of the Day, and the link is the chapter's design, and the design is the single Day that all the chapter's warnings have been pointing to, and the pointing is the verse's final work in the scene.",
  "102": "\"As for those whose scale is heavy ˹with good deeds˺, it is they who will be successful\" opens the chapter's scene of the scales, and the verse is the first verdict of the weighing, and the verdict is the success, and the success is the chapter's first word and its last, and the word is the verse's content. The verse states the first side: \"As for those whose scale is heavy ˹with good deeds˺,\" and the word \"scale\" is 'mawazin,' the word of the balances, and the verse says the heavy scale is the good deeds', and the commentators explain the meaning: the weighing of the deeds is the Day's justice, and the heavy is the believer's, and the verse then states the verdict: \"it is they who will be successful\", and the word \"successful\" is 'muflihun,' the word of the harvest's owners, and the verse says the heavy are the successful, and the word is the chapter's opening repeated, and the commentators note the echo: the chapter began with \"Successful indeed are the believers\", and the verse at the end returns the word, and the return is the chapter's design, and the design is the circle, and the circle is the verse's teaching on the success, and the teaching is the next verse's contrast, and the contrast is the light scale, and the light scale is the chapter's next teaching. The same weighing is described elsewhere: \"The weighing on that Day will be just. As for those whose scale will be heavy ˹with good deeds˺, ˹only˺ they will be successful\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the weighing in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the scales, and the scales are the chapter's destination. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the circle, and the teaching is the echo, and the echo is the chapter's word on the success, and the word is that the success of the opening is the success of the weighing, and the weighing is the chapter's answer to the doubt, and the answer is the heavy scale, and the heavy scale is the chapter's final word on the believer.",
  "103": "\"But those whose scale is light, they will have doomed themselves, staying in Hell forever\" states the second verdict of the weighing, and the verse is the contrast of the previous verse, and the contrast is the chapter's method, and the method is the two sides, and the two sides are the verse's content. The verse states the second side: \"But those whose scale is light,\" and the word \"light\" is 'khaffat,' the word of the weightless, and the verse says the light scale is the deniers', and the commentators explain the meaning: the scale that carries no good is the light, and the verse then states the verdict: \"they will have doomed themselves, staying in Hell forever\", and the word \"doomed\" is 'khasiru,' the word of the losing, and the verse says the light have lost themselves, and the commentators explain the word's force: the loss is not of the goods but of the self, and the verse then adds the stay: \"staying in Hell forever\", and the word \"forever\" is 'khalidun,' the word of the endless, and the verse says the stay is endless, and the endless is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the loss, and the teaching is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the Fire's burning, and the burning is the chapter's next teaching. The same weighing is described elsewhere: \"But those whose scale is light, they have doomed themselves for wrongfully denying Our signs\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the contrast in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the two sides, and the two sides are the chapter's destination. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the loss, and the teaching is the self, and the self is the chapter's word on the light scale, and the word is that the loss is the self's, and the self's loss is the chapter's answer to the gain, and the answer is the endless stay, and the endless stay is the chapter's final word on the denier.",
  "104": "\"The Fire will burn their faces, leaving them deformed\" describes the state of the light-scaled in the Fire, and the verse is the chapter's picture of the punishment, and the picture is the face, and the face is the verse's content. The verse states the burning: \"The Fire will burn their faces, leaving them deformed\", and the word \"deformed\" is 'kalihun,' the word of the grimacing, and the commentators explain the meaning: the faces are burned until the grimace is fixed, and the fixing is the verse's picture, and the picture is the chapter's teaching on the punishment's depth, and the teaching is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the pride, and the answer is the face, and the face is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the pride's end, and the end is the grimace, and the grimace is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's transition to the dialogue, and the transition is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the question, and the question is the chapter's next teaching. The same burning is described elsewhere: \"On this Day We will seal their mouths, their hands will speak to Us, and their feet will testify to what they used to commit\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the burning in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the Fire, and the Fire is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the chapter's remaining verses. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the face, and the teaching is the grimace, and the grimace is the chapter's word on the pride, and the word is that the pride ends in the grimace, and the ending is the chapter's answer to the arrogance, and the answer is the burning, and the burning is the chapter's final word on the Fire.",
  "105": "\"˹It will be said,˺\"\"Were My revelations not recited to you, but you used to deny them?\" opens the chapter's dialogue of the Fire, and the verse is the question that will be asked, and the question is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the verse's content. The verse quotes the question: \"Were My revelations not recited to you, but you used to deny them?\" and the question has two halves, and the two are the record and the verdict, and the commentators explain the meaning: the question reminds the deniers of the recitation, and the reminder is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is that the revelations were recited, and the reciting was the door, and the verse then names the response: \"but you used to deny them\", and the word \"deny\" is 'tukadhdhibun,' the word of the calling false, and the verse says the denial was the response, and the saying is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the closed door, and the teaching is that the denial closed what the recitation opened, and the verse's question is the chapter's warning, and the warning is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the cry, and the cry is the chapter's next teaching. The same question is described elsewhere: \"This record of Ours speaks the truth about you. Indeed, We always had your deeds recorded ˹by the angels˺\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the question in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the dialogue, and the dialogue is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the chapter's remaining verses. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the question, and the teaching is the record, and the record is the chapter's word on the recitation, and the word is that the recitation was the door, and the door is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the next verses' cry, and the cry is the chapter's final word on the scene.",
  "106": "\"They will cry,\"\"Our Lord! Our ill-fate took hold of us, so we became a misguided people\" records the deniers' answer to the question, and the verse is the chapter's picture of the excuse, and the picture is the ill-fate, and the ill-fate is the verse's content. The verse quotes the cry: \"Our Lord! Our ill-fate took hold of us, so we became a misguided people\", and the word \"ill-fate\" is 'shiqwatuna,' the word of the wretchedness, and the verse says the deniers blame the wretchedness, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers' excuse is the fate, and the excuse is the chapter's picture of the blame-shifting, and the picture is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the excuse, and the teaching is that the wretchedness was their own deeds, and the verse then quotes the conclusion: \"so we became a misguided people\", and the word \"misguided\" is the word of the straying, and the verse says the deniers admit the straying, and the admission is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the confession, and the teaching is that the confession is true and late, and the verse's two words together are the chapter's picture of the cry, and the picture is the next verse's setup, and the setup is the request, and the request is the chapter's next teaching. The same excuse is recorded elsewhere: \"They argued, \"Our forefathers had associated others with Allah. We are only their descendants,\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the excuse in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the dialogue, and the dialogue is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the chapter's remaining verses. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the excuse, and the teaching is the ill-fate, and the ill-fate is the chapter's word on the blame, and the word is that the blame is the shifting, and the shifting is the chapter's answer to the confession, and the answer is the next verse's request, and the request is the chapter's final word on the appeal.",
  "107": "\"Our Lord! Take us out of this ˹Fire˺. Then if we ever return ˹to denial˺, we will truly be wrongdoers\" completes the cry, and the verse is the request, and the request is the chapter's picture of the appeal, and the appeal is the verse's content. The verse quotes the request: \"Our Lord! Take us out of this ˹Fire˺\", and the word behind the request \"Take us out\" is 'akhrijna,' the word of the bringing out, and the verse says the deniers ask for the exit, and the asking is the chapter's picture of the appeal, and the picture is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the deathbed's cry repeated, and the teaching is that the appeal is the same, and the verse then quotes the promise: \"Then if we ever return ˹to denial˺, we will truly be wrongdoers\", and the word \"promise\" is the verse's irony, and the commentators explain the meaning: the deniers promise the obedience if they are returned, and the promise is the chapter's record of the appeal's last form, and the form is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the appeal's emptiness, and the teaching is the next verse's answer, and the answer is the refusal, and the refusal is the chapter's next teaching. The same request is recorded elsewhere: \"There they will be ˹fervently˺ screaming, \"Our Lord! Take us out ˹and send us back˺. We will do good, unlike what we used to do!\"\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the request in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the dialogue, and the dialogue is the chapter's destination. The verse's record of the promise is also the chapter's picture of the appeal's last form, and the form is the bargaining, and the bargaining is the chapter's teaching on the appeal's emptiness, and the emptiness is the next verse's answer, and the answer is the scolding.",
  "108": "\"Allah will respond,\"\"Be despised in there! Do not ˹ever˺ plead with Me ˹again˺!\" records the answer to the appeal, and the verse is the refusal, and the refusal is the chapter's teaching on the closed door, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the answer: \"Be despised in there!\" and the word \"despised\" is 'ikhsa'u,' the word of the scolding, and the commentators explain the meaning: the word is the harshest of the refusals, and the harshness is the chapter's picture of the answer, and the picture is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the appeal's end, and the teaching is that the appeal is met with the scolding, and the verse then quotes the seal: \"Do not ˹ever˺ plead with Me ˹again˺!\" and the word \"plead\" is 'tukallimun,' the word of the speaking, and the verse says the pleading is closed, and the closing is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the door, and the teaching is that the door was the world, and the world is gone, and the verse's two words together are the chapter's answer to the deathbed's cry, and the answer is the \"Never\" of the earlier verse repeated, and the repetition is the chapter's design, and the design is the closed door, and the closed door is the chapter's warning, and the warning is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the remembrance, and the remembrance is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the door, and the teaching is the scolding, and the scolding is the chapter's word on the appeal, and the word is that the appeal is closed, and the closing is the chapter's answer to the deathbed, and the answer is the next verses' record, and the record is the chapter's final word on the two groups.",
  "109": "\"Indeed, there was a group of My servants who used to pray,\"'Our Lord! We have believed, so forgive us and have mercy on us, for You are the best of those who show mercy,' continues the answer, and the verse turns from the refusal to the record, and the record is the chapter's teaching on the two groups, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the record: \"Indeed, there was a group of My servants who used to pray,\" and the word \"group\" is 'fariq,' the word of the company, and the verse says the company existed, and the commentators explain the meaning: the believers were among the deniers, and the deniers saw them, and the verse then quotes the prayer: \"Our Lord! We have believed, so forgive us and have mercy on us, for You are the best of those who show mercy,\" and the prayer is the chapter's picture of the believers' word, and the picture is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the believers' manner, and the teaching is the asking, and the asking is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's contrast to the deniers' appeal, and the contrast is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the two prayers, and the teaching is that the believers asked in the world, and the deniers ask in the Fire, and the two askings are the two groups, and the two groups are the next verse's subject, and the subject is the mockery, and the mockery is the chapter's next teaching. The same prayer is recorded elsewhere: \"Our Lord! We have wronged ourselves. If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will certainly be losers\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the prayer in the record, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the dialogue, and the dialogue is the chapter's destination.",
  "110": "\"but you were ˹so busy˺ making fun of them that it made you forget My remembrance. And you used to laugh at them\" states the charge against the deniers, and the verse is the chapter's record of the mockery, and the record is the verse's content. The verse addresses the deniers: \"but you were ˹so busy˺ making fun of them that it made you forget My remembrance\", and the word \"making fun\" is 'sikhriyyan,' the word of the mocking, and the verse says the mockery of the believers was the deniers' occupation, and the commentators explain the meaning: the mockery filled their time until the remembrance of God was forgotten, and the forgetting is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the mockery's cost, and the teaching is the forgetting, and the verse then adds the picture: \"And you used to laugh at them\", and the word \"laugh\" is 'tadhakun,' the word of the laughing, and the verse says the laughter was the deniers' response to the believers, and the picture is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the mockery's end, and the teaching is that the laughter is now the Fire's, and the verse's two words together are the chapter's answer to the deniers' appeal, and the answer is the record of the mockery, and the record is the chapter's warning to every mocker, and the warning is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the reward, and the reward is the chapter's next teaching. The same charge is recorded elsewhere: \"Indeed, the wicked used to laugh at the believers\", winking to one another whenever they passed by. and the verse of the chapter keeps the charge in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the dialogue, and the dialogue is the chapter's destination.",
  "111": "\"Today I have indeed rewarded them for their perseverance: they are certainly the triumphant\" states the reward of the mocked, and the verse is the chapter's verdict on the two groups, and the verdict is the triumph, and the triumph is the verse's content. The verse quotes the verdict: \"Today I have indeed rewarded them for their perseverance\", and the word \"perseverance\" is 'sabaru,' the word of the patience, and the verse says the reward is for the patience, and the commentators explain the meaning: the believers' patience under the mockery is the deed, and the deed is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the patience, and the teaching is that the patience is the price, and the verse then states the result: \"they are certainly the triumphant\", and the word \"triumphant\" is 'fa'izun,' the word of the winners, and the verse says the mocked are the winners, and the saying is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the reversal, and the teaching is that the laughter is reversed, and the reversal is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's answer to the mockery, and the answer is the triumph, and the triumph is the chapter's design, and the design is the reversal, and the reversal is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the years, and the years are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the reversal, and the teaching is the triumph, and the triumph is the chapter's word on the patience, and the word is that the patience is the price of the reversal, and the price is the chapter's answer to the mockery, and the answer is the next verses' subject, and the subject is the years, and the years are the chapter's final teaching.",
  "112": "\"He will ask ˹them˺,\"\"How many years did you remain on earth?\" opens the chapter's final dialogue, and the verse is the question of the years, and the question is the chapter's teaching on the brevity, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the question: \"How many years did you remain on earth?\" and the word \"remain\" is 'labithtum,' the word of the staying, and the verse says the deniers will be asked about the length of their stay, and the commentators explain the meaning: the question is not for the knowledge but for the realization, and the realization is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the question's purpose, and the purpose is the awakening, and the verse then leaves the question hanging, and the hanging is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's design, and the design is the dialogue's setup, and the setup is the next verse's answer, and the answer is the day, and the day is the chapter's next teaching. The same question is described elsewhere: \"On the Day He will gather them, it will be as if they had not stayed ˹in the world˺ except for an hour of a day\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the question in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the dialogue, and the dialogue is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the chapter's final verses. The verse is the chapter's teaching on the question, and the teaching is the realization, and the realization is the chapter's word on the years, and the word is that the years are the question's subject, and the subject is the brevity, and the brevity is the chapter's final word on the life.",
  "113": "\"They will reply,\"\"We remained ˹only˺ a day or part of a day. But ask those who kept count\" records the answer to the question, and the verse is the chapter's picture of the brevity, and the picture is the day, and the day is the verse's content. The verse quotes the answer: \"We remained ˹only˺ a day or part of a day\", and the word \"day\" is 'yawman,' the word of the single day, and the verse says the deniers will measure their whole life as a day, and the commentators explain the meaning: the life that felt long in the world will feel like a day on the Day, and the feeling is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the brevity, and the teaching is the scale, and the verse then quotes the hesitation: \"But ask those who kept count\", and the word \"count\" is 'addin,' the word of the counting, and the verse says the deniers doubt their own estimate, and the doubt is the verse's second word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the confusion, and the teaching is that the Day confuses the measures, and the verse's two words together are the chapter's picture of the answer, and the picture is the next verse's setup, and the setup is the verdict, and the verdict is the chapter's next teaching. The same answer is recorded elsewhere: \"You stayed no more than ten days ˹on the earth˺\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the answer in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the dialogue, and the dialogue is the chapter's destination. The verse's hesitation is also the chapter's picture of the Day's confusion, and the confusion is the chapter's teaching on the measures, and the teaching is that the Day reverses the measures, and the reversal is the next verse's verdict, and the verdict is the chapter's final word on the brevity.",
  "114": "He will say, \"You only remained for a little while, if only you knew\" records the verdict on the answer, and the verse is the chapter's word on the brevity, and the word is the verse's content. The verse quotes the verdict: \"You only remained for a little while, if only you knew\", and the word \"little\" is 'qalilan,' the word of the small, and the verse says the stay was little, and the commentators explain the meaning: the verdict confirms the deniers' estimate and turns it into the lesson, and the verse then quotes the regret: \"if only you knew\", and the word \"knew\" is 'ta'lamun,' the word of the knowing, and the verse says the knowing is the wish, and the commentators explain the meaning: the wish is that the knowing had come in the world, and the wishing is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the regret, and the teaching is that the regret is the knowing's lateness, and the verse's two parts together are the chapter's answer to the question, and the answer is the brevity, and the brevity is the chapter's teaching on the life, and the teaching is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the purpose, and the purpose is the chapter's final teaching. The same verdict is recorded elsewhere: \"You stayed no more than a day\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the verdict in the scene, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the dialogue, and the dialogue is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the chapter's final verse. The verse's word 'little' is also the chapter's measure of the life, and the measure is the chapter's answer to the heedlessness, and the answer is that the life was the little, and the little was the chance, and the chance is the chapter's final teaching on the world.",
  "115": "\"Did you then think that We had created you without purpose, and that you would never be returned to Us?\"\" closes the chapter, and the verse is the final question, and the question is the chapter's whole teaching gathered into one line, and the line is the verse's content. The verse quotes the question: \"Did you then think that We had created you without purpose, and that you would never be returned to Us?\" and the question has two halves, and the two are the chapter's two great truths, and the commentators explain the meaning: the first half is the purpose, and the verse asks whether the creation was imagined to be without it, and the asking is the chapter's answer to the heedlessness, and the answer is the purpose, and the second half is the return, and the verse asks whether the return was imagined to be absent, and the asking is the chapter's answer to the denial, and the answer is the return, and the two halves together are the chapter's entire message, and the message is the verse's first word, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the creation's purpose, and the teaching is the verse's last word, and the word is the chapter's closing, and the closing is the question, and the question is the chapter's final gift to the reader, and the gift is the asking, and the asking is the reader's own, and the reader's answer is the chapter's destination, and the destination is the return, and the return is the chapter's final word. The same question is stated elsewhere: \"Do people think they will be left without purpose?\" and the verse of the chapter keeps the question at the end, and the keeping is the chapter's design, and the design is the circle, and the circle is the chapter's completion, and the completion is the success of the opening, and the success is the believers', and the believers are the chapter's first word and its last.",
  "116": "\"Exalted is Allah, the True King! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him, the Lord of the Honourable Throne\" closes the chapter of the believers, and the closing is the answer to the final question that the previous verse asked, the question of whether the creation was made without purpose, and the answer is this declaration, and the declaration has three parts, and the three are the chapter's creed in its fullest form. The first part is the exaltation: \"Exalted is Allah, the True King!\" and the word \"Exalted\" is 'ta'ala,' the word of the rising above, and the verse says God is above every description that falls short, and the title \"the True King\" is 'al-Malik al-Haqq,' and the commentators explain the meaning: every king of this world is a borrower of his crown, and God is the King whose kingship is real, and the word \"true\" separates the real King from the shadows of kingship, and the exaltation is the verse's first stroke, and the same opening appears elsewhere in the Quran: \"Exalted is Allah, the True King!\" The second part is the oneness: \"There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him,\" and the verse states the creed in its shortest form, the word that has run through the whole chapter from the calls of Noah and every messenger, and the verse places the oneness at the chapter's end where it placed it at every story's heart, and the oneness is the verse's second stroke. The third part is the Throne: \"the Lord of the Honourable Throne\", and the word \"Honourable\" is 'karim,' the word of the noble, and the verse names the Throne with the quality of honour, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Throne is the highest of the creation, and its Lord is the True King, and the naming of the Throne gathers the whole creation under the one King, and the gathering is the verse's third stroke, and the three strokes together are the chapter's answer to every false king and every false god, and the answer is the closing, and the closing is the chapter's creed, and the same Throne is described elsewhere as the \"Mighty Throne\", and the two names of the Throne, the mighty and the honourable, are the two faces of the one Kingship.",
  "117": "\"Whoever invokes, besides Allah, another god—for which they can have no proof—they will surely find their penalty with their Lord. Indeed, the disbelievers will never succeed\" states the chapter's verdict on the false gods, and the verse is the last warning of the chapter, and the warning is built on the word \"proof,\" and the proof is the verse's content. The verse begins with the act: \"Whoever invokes, besides Allah, another god,\" and the word \"invokes\" is 'yad'u,' the word of the calling, and the verse says the calling is of another god, and the calling is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's answer to the idolatry of the stories, and the answer is the record of the act, and the verse then states the groundlessness: \"for which they can have no proof\", and the word \"proof\" is 'burhan,' the word of the clear evidence, and the verse says the proof is absent, and the commentators explain the meaning: the false god has no warrant, and the chapter has made the same argument elsewhere: \"Show ˹me˺ your proof,\" and the argument is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the false god, and the teaching is that the false god is the proof-less, and the verse then states the end: \"they will surely find their penalty with their Lord\", and the word \"penalty\" is 'hisabuhu,' the word of the account, and the verse says the account is with their Lord, and the commentators explain the meaning: the account is the reckoning of the Day, and the verse says the reckoning is certain, and the certainty is the verse's third stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's warning to the idolater, and the verse then closes with the verdict: \"Indeed, the disbelievers will never succeed\", and the word \"succeed\" is 'yuflihu,' the word of the harvest, and the verse says the disbelievers will not harvest, and the word is the chapter's last echo of its first word, and the echo is the verse's final stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design: the chapter opened with \"Successful indeed are the believers\", and it closes with the disbelievers' failure, and between the two the whole chapter has run, and the running is the chapter's teaching, and the teaching is the verse's whole content.",
  "118": "\"Say, ˹O Prophet,˺\"\"My Lord! Forgive and have mercy, for You are the best of those who show mercy\" closes the chapter with a prayer, and the prayer is the chapter's last word, and the word is the asking, and the asking is the verse's content. The verse instructs the Prophet to pray: \"My Lord! Forgive and have mercy,\" and the prayer has two halves, and the two are the complete asking, and the commentators explain the meaning: the forgiveness is the covering of the sins, and the mercy is the gift that follows the covering, and the two together are the believer's whole need, and the verse places the two in the chapter's end, and the placement is the chapter's teaching: the chapter that described the believers' qualities and the deniers' fates ends with the believers' prayer, and the prayer is the answer to everything the chapter described, and the answer is the asking. The verse then states the ground of the asking: \"for You are the best of those who show mercy,\" and the word \"best\" is 'khayr,' and the verse says God is the best of the merciful, and the commentators note the chapter's echo, and the echo is the verse's deepest point: the same words were quoted earlier in the chapter as the prayer of the believers whom the deniers mocked: \"Our Lord! We have believed, so forgive us and have mercy on us, for You are the best of those who show mercy,\" and the chapter that recorded their prayer in the midst of the mockery now ends by placing the same prayer on the Prophet's own tongue, and the placement is the chapter's design: the mocked prayer is the chapter's closing prayer, and the closing is the chapter's answer to the mockery, and the answer is that the prayer of the believers became the prayer of the Prophet, and the prayer is the chapter's last word. The same asking is recorded in the prayer of Adam: \"Our Lord! We have wronged ourselves. If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will certainly be losers\", and the verse of the chapter keeps the asking in its simplest form, and the form is the chapter's teaching on the believer's end: the believer ends as he lived, asking, and the asking is the believer's own, and the chapter that began with the success of the believers ends with their asking, and the two ends hold the whole religion, the success and the mercy, and the mercy is the chapter's final word."
};
